WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.492 --> 00:00:14.700
PRESENTER: Mr. Rubbia has
a distinguished high energy

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.742
physics career, so he
needs a little introduction

00:00:16.742 --> 00:00:18.880
for any physicists
who are around here.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.370
But, since we're having
this talk at the Media Lab,

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:21.953
we probably have a
more general crowd,

00:00:21.953 --> 00:00:24.638
so I'll give a
quick introduction.

00:00:24.638 --> 00:00:26.430
Even if you're not a
high energy physicist,

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.222
you've probably heard
about his famous work

00:00:28.222 --> 00:00:30.130
at CERN in the early 1980s.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:33.150
He's been a major innovator
behind the world's first proton

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:36.170
and deproton collider there,
the CERN P-bar-P collider.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.040
There, he led the
UA1 experiment,

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.492
a very large at
that time physics

00:00:39.492 --> 00:00:41.700
collaboration that discovered
there are quantum field

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.790
particles that mediate the weak
interaction, the Ws and the Z.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:46.227
He won the Nobel
Prize in physics

00:00:46.227 --> 00:00:48.810
for this work, shared with Simon
van der Meer, the accelerator

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.448
expert, in 1984.

00:00:50.448 --> 00:00:52.740
He has been the Higgins
Professor of Physics at Harvard

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:53.947
during that time.

00:00:53.947 --> 00:00:56.280
In the early '90s, he was the
director general of CERN--

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.947
played a prime role
in shaping the Large

00:00:57.947 --> 00:01:01.310
Hadron Collider, the main
accelerator of this decade--

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:03.310
turning on in the middle
of the decade, we hope.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.037
RUBBIA: Don't fall asleep.

00:01:05.037 --> 00:01:07.120
PRESENTER: And he's active
in many projects since,

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.037
including the ICARUS--

00:01:08.037 --> 00:01:09.090
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.010
PRESENTER: --a
neutron [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:14.158
neutrino detector under the
Gran Sasso, outside of Rome,

00:01:14.158 --> 00:01:16.200
and other things, including
the energy amplifier,

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.200
a novel and safe way of
producing nuclear energy

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.310
exploiting present-day
accelerator technologies,

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.560
and what he's going to
talk to us about today,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.030
an extrapolation
of these concepts

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.550
to exciting new prospects
for nuclear rocketry.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:27.300
Professor Rubbia.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.320
RUBBIA: Thank you.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.570
How do you turn this thing on?

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:29.940
It must be--

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:30.900
MODERATOR: [INAUDIBLE] switch.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:31.830
RUBBIA: Just a simple switch!

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:32.580
That's it.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:33.840
Amazing.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.932
Things tend to be
complicated, around here.

00:01:35.932 --> 00:01:38.140
Well, first of all, I don't
know what I'm doing here.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.320
I don't know what you're
doing here, with me.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.980
But I had a very nice
afternoon, looking at all many,

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.350
all different, crazy ideas--

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.520
and unthinkable ideas.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:55.780
And so I feel rather prepared to
tell you about my ideas, which

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:58.530
are not that different
[INAUDIBLE] one

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:02.678
you are tossing around here.

00:02:02.678 --> 00:02:04.470
Maybe something probably
will never happen,

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:08.590
it will never be realized,
but is worth trying.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:11.910
I learned that 10% of your
ideas come true to reality.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.430
Well, it's a good coefficient.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:17.740
If we can do the same with this,
I think we'll be in good shape.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:21.040
So anyway, the
problem is [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.155
about getting to Mars.

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:28.480
And connected with
it is the question

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.140
of whether nuclear power
could be used for that.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:35.650
There is a group
of people in Europe

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.540
working quite hard on this.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:39.887
It's about-- it's been growing.

00:02:39.887 --> 00:02:41.470
At the beginning,
we had a few people.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:46.540
Now it's [INAUDIBLE] to, oh,
I would say, 50, 60 people,

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.150
working on these
kind of concepts--

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:50.350
very general concepts.

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:52.760
They have no practical
connection, for the moment,

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:57.860
but they show the need
for some new ideas.

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:01.480
In fact, the need for new ideas
is pretty evident [INAUDIBLE]..

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:05.650
You know that the initial
description of going to Mars

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:09.580
has been done by von Braun,
in the famous early book which

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:14.950
he wrote in German when
he was a war prisoner.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:19.270
And in that he described
the standard mechanism

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:20.840
to get there.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:24.690
There are a lot of
difficulty, in doing this,

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:25.620
at the present moment.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:29.960
Many people, including
especially NASA,

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:31.840
is trying to get there.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.270
It's not very simple.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.380
But eventually it
would seem to me

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:38.830
that this is one of the things
mankind would like to do.

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:40.470
Surely I will not be
there to see that,

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:45.720
but I think it's still
fun to think about it.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.410
Now, if you wish to
do this kind of jump,

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:53.070
from going to the moon,
300,000 kilometers,

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:56.700
to going on to Mars,
where the round trip is

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.540
1 billion kilometers, you
have to somehow introduce

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:01.960
some substantial change.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:04.415
You just cannot use the
same technology over again.

00:04:04.415 --> 00:04:06.540
And the real question is,
can you use, or would you

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:08.940
like to use--

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:12.520
This projector's not
up to MIT standards.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:13.930
Hmm?

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:16.250
It's totally out of focus.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:17.610
And there's a lot of operations.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:20.180
Okay.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:22.115
I would say, up to
Harvard standards.

00:04:22.115 --> 00:04:23.570
[LAUGH]

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:28.203
Anyway, so the question
is, do you have to use--

00:04:28.203 --> 00:04:29.370
can you use-- nuclear power?

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:31.997
And I think you can
certainly convince yourself

00:04:31.997 --> 00:04:33.830
nuclear power is
[INAUDIBLE] is interesting,

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:36.320
because it offers
you 3 million times

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.630
more specific energy than
the chemical reactions,

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:40.640
when you burn it all.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:43.770
But energy's not the problem,
when, you travel in space.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:48.290
What's important, in space
propulsion, is impulse,

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:51.560
is momentum, is speed.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:53.910
And, in fact, there is
a famous rocket equation

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:57.230
which determines how much stuff
you can get there when you

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.092
start with this initial mass.

00:04:59.092 --> 00:05:01.550
And this is the ratio between--
it's a very simple equation

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:04.870
which contains, here, the
sum of the speed that you

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:08.090
have to add to the
various operations

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:12.470
and divides up v, speed
of exhaust of your gas.

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:18.680
And that is essentially
fixed by chemical fuels which

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:20.990
can reach temperatures
of that order

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:25.070
and provide the specific impulse
of the order of 450 seconds.

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:28.310
I don't know whether you know
what specific impulse is.

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:35.600
The specific impulse is,
essentially, the amount of time

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.730
as measured in seconds,
the amount of time

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:46.160
over which a fuel will apply
a thrust equal to its weight.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:49.340
So, if I have a kilogram
of a certain material,

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:54.000
and I burn it, that gives me
a kilogram for so much time.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.460
And that time is the
impulse, specific impulse.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:00.340
So, essentially, if you have
a kilogram of chemical fuel,

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:04.250
it provide you a kilogram
of thrust over 450 seconds.

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:06.120
And that is essentially
specific impulse,

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.480
which is proportional
to the speed of exhaust.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:13.250
Now, the idea is, of
course, to go nuclear.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:17.160
But the nuclear system is
limited in the temperature.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:20.390
No, you cannot even get to
the temperature of the gas.

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:23.410
And because essentially
your material--

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:27.800
so your reactor is not standing
the high temperature itself.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:29.660
And so you do with
a thing called

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:34.220
[INAUDIBLE] developed in Russia,
in the United States, 900

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:34.720
seconds.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:37.580
But you do that essentially
not because you have a higher

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.880
temperature but simply
because you're using hydrogen.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:43.700
And hydrogen has this
lower atomic number,

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.280
so molecules move
faster, and therefore

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:48.920
you get higher speed of exhaust.

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:54.180
Now, in other words, this is
a little bit the situation.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.330
If you look at the
standard-- here

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:58.160
is the stagnation
temperature, temperature

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:01.130
which your system is operating.

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:02.680
Here is liquid
hydrogen and oxygen--

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:08.970
gets you an exhaust speed of
about 4 kilometers per second--

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:12.570
So, a specific
impulse about 450.

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:15.950
And we are talking about
machines, or, if you like,

00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:19.290
systems, which should allow
you to get to temperatures

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:22.010
which are typical in the order
between 10,000 and 15,000

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:23.210
degrees.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:27.140
This is hydrogen [INAUDIBLE]
and the specific impulses,

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:32.145
which are in the region
between 2,500 and maybe 4,000.

00:07:32.145 --> 00:07:34.520
So we are talking about an
order-of-magnitude improvement

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:39.650
in the specific impulse,
respect to this system,

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:42.410
based with normal
chemical system.

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:44.550
Factor 10 in speed
means a factor of 100

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:45.560
in specific energy.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:47.370
Let's be clear on that.

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:51.490
And that, of course, makes
the situation much easier

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:54.100
when you try to go to Mars.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:58.700
In fact, the
most-used trajectory

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:00.565
is so-called
minimum-energy trajectory.

00:08:00.565 --> 00:08:02.460
It's called the
Hohmann trajectory,

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:05.690
the one which was
proposed initially

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:10.220
by the original thing--

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:13.390
document-- book I was
telling you before.

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:14.390
The travel time is long.

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:19.070
It's 280 days it's essentially
freefall trajectory,

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:20.760
as you'll see in a minute.

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:23.520
However, if you get there and
then you want to come back,

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.530
you have to make sure that the
planets are in the right place.

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:30.370
And that means that you have to
add another 460 days residence.

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:32.799
So there's a fixed trajectory,
which you go for a year,

00:08:32.799 --> 00:08:34.230
you stay there a
year and a half,

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:35.968
and then you come
back for a year.

00:08:35.968 --> 00:08:37.760
And if you miss the
opportunity, of course,

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:39.593
things are not the right
place, and then you

00:08:39.593 --> 00:08:40.400
don't get anywhere.

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:43.100
Now, if you want to get a
little faster with this,

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:48.320
you have to catch up on
the motion of the Earth.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:50.772
And that in fact requires
much higher transfer speed.

00:08:50.772 --> 00:08:52.230
And I will explain
you what I mean.

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:57.920
Now, let's look at
this curve here.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.200
You see here you
have essentially

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:03.680
represented here the
[INAUDIBLE] of residence time.

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:10.360
And you have the 460, 640
days there, which correspond--

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:12.650
640 days, which
corresponds to the number

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:15.650
I gave you before, 540 days.

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:18.530
And then you see, here,
the various-- this

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:20.300
is the Hohmann trajectory.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:23.660
And then you see, all
these lines are possible.

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:28.550
They're characterized, of
course, by faster transfer

00:09:28.550 --> 00:09:31.190
velocity, but they give
you the same residence

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:34.520
time, which is of the
order of a couple of years.

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:39.343
If you want to go to a
much shorter time mission,

00:09:39.343 --> 00:09:41.010
you have to make
conjunction trajectory.

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:43.260
In other words, you have to
run after the Earth, which

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:44.820
is escaping away from you.

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:48.620
And that implies that you have
to go to much faster speeds.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:50.120
This is the area
which is permitted.

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:51.495
There is no other
way in between.

00:09:51.495 --> 00:09:53.530
So, if you have
very slow motions,

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:56.950
5 kilometers per second, then
you will be forced to do that.

00:09:56.950 --> 00:10:01.360
If you want to get to stay
there for [INAUDIBLE] less time,

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:04.630
you are obliged to go to
speeds, transfer speeds,

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:06.400
which are in fact quite large--

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:09.050
25, 30 kilometers per second.

00:10:09.050 --> 00:10:14.200
Which implies, of course,
speeds of your ejected gas

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:16.157
[INAUDIBLE] order of
magnitude and therefore

00:10:16.157 --> 00:10:16.990
require new methods.

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:19.060
We are talking
about, now, missions

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:21.580
of this type for the
manned CLIPPER missions.

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:25.510
And [INAUDIBLE] stay
there for the case

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:28.810
of transporting materials.

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:34.260
Now, let me show you briefly how
this mission can be realized.

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:36.620
The fast mission,
as I said, consists

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:42.050
of leaving the Earth here and
then running on this trajectory

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:46.370
and getting here, after
150 days, on Mars.

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:50.630
Then you stay on Mars as long
as you wish, from 0 to 100 days

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:51.835
or whatever is required.

00:10:51.835 --> 00:10:53.210
And then you decide
to come back.

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:55.940
If you want to come
back, this time the Earth

00:10:55.940 --> 00:10:59.390
has gone further, so you have
to catch up the Earth position.

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:02.600
So you have to run
much closer to the Sun

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:04.910
and then make a turn inwards.

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:08.870
And that allows you to get back
even something like, as I say,

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:12.110
another 179 days, but--

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:15.130
so, therefore, you can close
the whole thing in a year.

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:20.380
But it requires speeds of,
as I said, much higher.

00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:23.180
This is for the people
which want to get there.

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:26.200
Now, if you want to
go and send equipment,

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:27.833
as you probably need
anyway, then you

00:11:27.833 --> 00:11:29.500
can follow the Hohmann
trajectory, which

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:31.880
is the one represented here.

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:34.240
And you can see what Hohmann
trajectory is all about,

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:35.650
is half a circle.

00:11:35.650 --> 00:11:36.430
You start here.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:40.660
You go in 280 days
to Mars, 260 days.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:43.870
Then you have to
stay all this time,

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:47.280
until such a time as the Earth
is in the right position.

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:50.620
And you leave here, and then you
go another Hohmann trajectory,

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:52.930
back 180 degrees [INAUDIBLE],,
and you hit the Earth

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:54.450
where it belongs.

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:58.770
That is, in fact,
the long-time trip.

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:02.120
Now, we are trying to study
all kinds of different missions

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:04.370
with the potential challenge
of this new engine, which

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:05.870
I'll describe in a minute.

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:08.590
Essentially, nuclear
power is going

00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:11.540
[INAUDIBLE] to be used only
for transplanetary trip.

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:13.550
Classical method for
landing and takes-off,

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:18.530
so you use standard fuel to get
to orbit and then to come down.

00:12:18.530 --> 00:12:21.457
Air braking is being
removed, at this level.

00:12:21.457 --> 00:12:23.540
Air braking is an extremely
complicated procedure.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:25.165
Imagine you arrive
on Mars and then you

00:12:25.165 --> 00:12:30.020
have to get into sort of an
orbit which is slowing down

00:12:30.020 --> 00:12:30.920
into the gas of Mars.

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:32.670
And the window is very narrow.

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:34.880
The difficulties of doing
that are considerable.

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:37.770
In fact, many of the problems
in getting on Mars material

00:12:37.770 --> 00:12:39.170
come from that point.

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:43.430
We think that, with people,
you should avoid it.

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:46.940
The space station ALPHA
is used as a departure.

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:51.650
And [INAUDIBLE] mission
needs a reusable shuttle,

00:12:51.650 --> 00:12:54.230
since many trips
are necessary and--

00:12:54.230 --> 00:12:55.410
could be necessary.

00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:57.830
And this device
will in fact operate

00:12:57.830 --> 00:13:03.420
on a periodicity of Earth-Mars
orbits, which is 740 days.

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:05.390
And the question is,
the presence on Mars.

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:09.290
So the man is necessary for
the complexity of the task,

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:12.410
but the harsh environment
limits a stay to a short time.

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:15.230
It's inconceivable you can leave
a person for first time on Mars

00:13:15.230 --> 00:13:17.397
one and a half years
eventually to make his own fuel

00:13:17.397 --> 00:13:19.045
and come back and so forth.

00:13:19.045 --> 00:13:20.420
So he has to be
able to go there,

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:25.910
to get the equipment going,
but then he has to come back.

00:13:25.910 --> 00:13:30.012
And you need no sharp
departure window.

00:13:30.012 --> 00:13:32.220
And then you have two kind
of missions-- the CLIPPER,

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:33.880
for fast manned
transit, and the TUG,

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:37.720
for material transportation,
which can be slow.

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:42.850
And the key, of course, all that
is a higher specific impulse.

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:46.620
Now, the present state
of nuclear propulsion

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:48.780
is not a very good state.

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:54.780
Essentially, Nerva is the design
which is now the best in town.

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:58.230
And it's an essentially
gas-core fast reactor.

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:00.360
The maximum temperature
is determined

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:05.100
by the temperature of this
uranium zirconium niobium

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:07.740
carbide, which has
to remain solid.

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:10.850
It runs at high
pressure, because the gas

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:13.503
has to go from here to there.

00:14:13.503 --> 00:14:14.670
Here is the low temperature.

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:18.000
Here is 3,000 degrees,
so it has to warm up,

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:20.400
going through the
rods of the reactors--

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:22.410
so, high operating pressure.

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:24.300
The mass of the
fuel must be large.

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:26.950
It's 1 ton of highly
enriched uranium,

00:14:26.950 --> 00:14:30.390
which is put into orbit in
order to get criticality.

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:35.280
But, however, during the
burning of all this system,

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:36.550
only 5 grams are burned.

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:38.405
The rest is-- it
just goes there.

00:14:38.405 --> 00:14:39.280
You have to deliver--

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:41.010
get rid of it.

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:43.278
And there are particular--

00:14:43.278 --> 00:14:44.820
many people getting
very nervous when

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:48.000
you put a ton of highly
enriched, bomb-grade uranium

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:50.070
on orbit.

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:52.090
Neutron leakage is very large--

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:54.330
10 to the 19
neutrons per second.

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:56.835
So it makes it very hard
for people to stay around.

00:14:56.835 --> 00:14:57.820
It has to be shielded.

00:14:57.820 --> 00:15:00.220
And even when it gets
shielded, it is not trivial.

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:02.790
You can avoid that
kind of irradiation

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:04.890
from [INAUDIBLE] these fluxes.

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:06.570
And the real problem
is essentially

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:11.880
that the fuel is, in fact, the
hottest part in the system.

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:15.330
Now, the basic idea is, can we
keep the fuel cold and the gas

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:16.380
hot?

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:18.893
It's what you would like to do.

00:15:18.893 --> 00:15:20.560
The answer to that
is a very simple idea

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:23.070
which we are working
on, which is direct

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:25.900
heating by the
fission fragments.

00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:29.710
Now, let me remind you
that 88% of nuclear energy

00:15:29.710 --> 00:15:33.600
due to fission is carried by
two fission fragments, the rest

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:37.290
being additional
neutrons and gamma rays.

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:39.870
You have essentially
uranium 235.

00:15:39.870 --> 00:15:41.610
You swallow a neutron.

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:45.300
Uranium 235 starts
to vibrate, to move.

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:48.180
It separates, like
two drops of water.

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:52.330
You get two little droplets.

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:54.060
They're both positively charged.

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:56.040
Therefore, they
repel each other.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:58.080
No longer nuclear
forces are present,

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:01.490
because you have severed
the connection with the two.

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:04.040
And the 200 MeV you
recover is just simply

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:06.700
the coolant repulsion
of these two parts.

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:08.340
So two fission
fragments come off,

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:13.860
and they carry essentially 98%
of the total kinetic energy.

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:14.938
So why not use--

00:16:14.938 --> 00:16:16.980
in addition, there are a
few gammas and neutrons,

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:19.350
but they carry only a few MeV.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:21.690
So most of the energy is
carried by these two fission

00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:24.550
fragments, which are back
to back, so to speak.

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:28.080
Now the question is, how can
you make use of that fission

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:28.830
fragment directly?

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:30.518
They have an enormous
enthalpy inside.

00:16:30.518 --> 00:16:32.310
They represent tremendous
high temperature.

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:34.115
But you never see
that mini reactor,

00:16:34.115 --> 00:16:35.490
because it stop
into the reactor.

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:38.950
Then you get warming
up of your material.

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:42.440
You don't get access
to that beauty

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:46.350
that nature is providing
you, there, with.

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:49.870
And so the question is,
can you use them directly

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:51.500
to heat up the gas?

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:53.500
The penetration range of
those fission fragments

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:55.330
is extremely short.

00:16:55.330 --> 00:16:57.730
It's 10 microns, in the fuel.

00:16:57.730 --> 00:16:59.560
And usually the
energy is converted

00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:01.690
in heat inside the fuel.

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:04.450
And now the question-- can
you use directly these fission

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:06.339
fragments in order
to heat the gas?

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:09.079
That will, of course, give
you an enormous advantage,

00:17:09.079 --> 00:17:10.730
as you'll see in a minute.

00:17:10.730 --> 00:17:13.240
So we assume a very
thin fissile deposit.

00:17:13.240 --> 00:17:14.920
Let's take a foil--

00:17:14.920 --> 00:17:18.880
put on a foil a layer
of fissionable material.

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:21.609
And then make it thin enough so
that the fission fragment can

00:17:21.609 --> 00:17:23.260
come out.

00:17:23.260 --> 00:17:25.329
And then see what
happens, as a function

00:17:25.329 --> 00:17:26.990
of the thickness of the foil.

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:30.550
Here you have a plot
which says 1 micron, 1

00:17:30.550 --> 00:17:32.810
milligram per square
centimeter, 10 milligram

00:17:32.810 --> 00:17:34.510
per square centimeters.

00:17:34.510 --> 00:17:37.820
These foil are made out
of uranium or americium

00:17:37.820 --> 00:17:39.810
or whatever you want.

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:42.310
And you see here the
energetic fraction

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:43.440
of the energy coming out.

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:47.930
Obviously you get 44%
if you had 0 thickness,

00:17:47.930 --> 00:17:51.910
because 88% is fission fragment
and 44 are going one direction,

00:17:51.910 --> 00:17:53.900
44 go to the other.

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:57.235
And then, when the thickness
becomes bigger, you get a loss.

00:17:57.235 --> 00:17:59.110
For instance, if you
have 1-micron thickness,

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:01.240
you get 34% instead of 44%.

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:03.490
If you go to 3 microns
milligrams per square

00:18:03.490 --> 00:18:04.978
centimeter, you get 24%.

00:18:04.978 --> 00:18:06.520
Now, what are we
talking about, here?

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:10.270
We are talking about 1 milligram
per square centimeter, which

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:13.360
is 0.8 microns in
thickness, which

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:18.550
is 10 grams of a
meter-squared surface.

00:18:18.550 --> 00:18:21.680
And we are talking about
1,100 atomic layers.

00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:23.955
This is certainly not what
you'd call a nuclear fuel.

00:18:23.955 --> 00:18:27.070
A nuclear fuel is a
very heavy material,

00:18:27.070 --> 00:18:29.940
hundreds of tons of
stuff inside the system.

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:32.810
Here is rather a radioactive
source, if you like,

00:18:32.810 --> 00:18:35.200
deposited on a thin layer.

00:18:35.200 --> 00:18:38.110
Now, how do you get criticality?

00:18:38.110 --> 00:18:40.855
Now, for that you need to
do some clever things, which

00:18:40.855 --> 00:18:41.980
have to do with neutronics.

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:44.950
Let me anticipate
to you that I do

00:18:44.950 --> 00:18:48.160
succeed in having criticality
with that little bit

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:49.780
of americium.

00:18:49.780 --> 00:18:51.640
Now, let me be
clear about numbers.

00:18:51.640 --> 00:18:54.180
If it take this americium,
which is the largest fission

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:58.598
cross-section known
around, 5,300 barns,

00:18:58.598 --> 00:19:00.140
and you get the
neutron through that,

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:02.938
it is 1% probability
of interacting.

00:19:02.938 --> 00:19:04.480
So, obviously, you
would like to have

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:07.690
100% probability of interacting,
to keep the reaction going.

00:19:07.690 --> 00:19:10.870
And therefore you need about
a factor of 100 enhancement.

00:19:10.870 --> 00:19:13.270
So can you enhance
the neutron flux,

00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:15.640
with some kind of storage
[INAUDIBLE] device,

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:18.550
so that you can get the
neutrons over and over again

00:19:18.550 --> 00:19:21.070
through your americium
foil and enhance,

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:24.770
this way, the
probability of fission?

00:19:24.770 --> 00:19:26.020
The answer is yes.

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:28.460
Well, we have done lots of
experiment at CERN on that.

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:31.310
And, in fact, this
is not just a theory.

00:19:31.310 --> 00:19:34.960
It's just an
experimental results.

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:38.020
The basic idea is that you
take a highly diffusing

00:19:38.020 --> 00:19:44.265
but neutron-transparent
medium, and you let

00:19:44.265 --> 00:19:47.728
in that thing a neutron travel.

00:19:47.728 --> 00:19:49.270
Only elastic
cross-sections is there.

00:19:49.270 --> 00:19:51.270
There is no inelasticity.

00:19:51.270 --> 00:19:54.390
So this neutron moves
around inside this material,

00:19:54.390 --> 00:19:58.350
in the same way that,
for instance, an electron

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:00.570
moves in an argon gas.

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:03.270
Well, the physical
reason's very similar.

00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:06.660
Normally, you're talking about
nuclei which are double-magic.

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:09.000
We have here the great
experts of these things.

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:13.280
And a double-magic nucleus
is like a noble gas.

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:14.800
The shell is closed.

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:18.460
So, in the same way that
an electron cannot capture

00:20:18.460 --> 00:20:22.230
in argon of helium, and it will
go around as a free electron

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:25.590
forever, here we are
talking about a nucleus.

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:28.900
And a neutron will not
capture the nucleus,

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:31.050
because the shell is closed.

00:20:31.050 --> 00:20:32.615
And the cross-section
is very small,

00:20:32.615 --> 00:20:34.740
and the result is, this
thing will just go around.

00:20:34.740 --> 00:20:37.020
Look here-- there's
[INAUDIBLE] tortuous path

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:38.820
inside the highly
efficient medium.

00:20:38.820 --> 00:20:40.950
This is computer
simulated, but the reality

00:20:40.950 --> 00:20:42.420
is not any different.

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:46.232
The neutron is essentially
stored, stored not by,

00:20:46.232 --> 00:20:48.190
like, a storage ring,
it's stored by diffusion.

00:20:48.190 --> 00:20:50.430
It's a random process
but, effectively, it

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:53.250
keeps the neutrons together.

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:55.920
And that is, in fact,
has been showed,

00:20:55.920 --> 00:21:01.150
that, in fact, 60-meters-long
path is wound in 35

00:21:01.150 --> 00:21:04.560
centimeters, 1 foot in length.

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:08.213
And this is due to the fact
that this movement [INAUDIBLE]..

00:21:08.213 --> 00:21:09.630
Now, in such a
storage medium-- so

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:14.040
you have a volume of this
type of high-purity graphite

00:21:14.040 --> 00:21:15.570
or high-purity lead.

00:21:15.570 --> 00:21:17.440
In that volume, you
have this phenomenon.

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:20.350
Such a storage medium,
because of multiple trials,

00:21:20.350 --> 00:21:23.400
even a very tiny
amount of impurity

00:21:23.400 --> 00:21:27.390
can absorb a significant
amount of capture elements.

00:21:27.390 --> 00:21:30.060
Suppose you have a
ball full of water.

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:32.790
And you put there a little
bit of ink, just a little drop

00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:35.700
of ink, the whole box will
have color of the ink,

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:38.010
even if the amount of
ink is infinitesimal.

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:40.670
Because the water is transparent
and is highly absorptive.

00:21:40.670 --> 00:21:44.230
And sooner or later light
will find the molecule ink

00:21:44.230 --> 00:21:47.840
and will send back the
color which you put in.

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:49.410
And same thing happens, here.

00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:53.937
You have a very small amount of
impurities, inside this medium,

00:21:53.937 --> 00:21:55.770
because you go around
and around and around,

00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:58.410
over and over again, you will
eventually hit an impurity,

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:01.890
and then you'll get a factor,
because of this multiple trial

00:22:01.890 --> 00:22:02.470
effect.

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:06.150
And therefore, 20 grams of
americium inside this box

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:07.990
will make it critical.

00:22:07.990 --> 00:22:09.550
So now you can
make devices which

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:12.370
are critical with grams of
material, not kilograms.

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:14.380
Remember, in the
Nerva, you needed

00:22:14.380 --> 00:22:20.350
one ton of highly fissile
bomb-grade uranium.

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:23.570
Here, with 20 grams of
americium, you're critical.

00:22:23.570 --> 00:22:25.777
In this continued medium.

00:22:25.777 --> 00:22:28.110
Now, of course, you don't
want to have continued medium.

00:22:28.110 --> 00:22:35.910
You want to have, essentially,
the way to do this fission.

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:40.230
So we introduce a statement
which we call Hohlraum,

00:22:40.230 --> 00:22:44.470
because it reminds
of a black body,

00:22:44.470 --> 00:22:47.910
which is essentially a box
which [INAUDIBLE] high-purity

00:22:47.910 --> 00:22:53.660
[INAUDIBLE] diffuser, on which
walls you put a thin layer--

00:22:53.660 --> 00:22:57.750
1 micron, 1,000 molecules
layer of americium.

00:22:57.750 --> 00:23:00.960
And then what happens--
neutrons will ping-pong

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:03.340
between the walls
of this container,

00:23:03.340 --> 00:23:06.030
will traverse many
times the walls.

00:23:06.030 --> 00:23:11.400
And [INAUDIBLE] enhance the
interaction probability,

00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:14.710
as we will see in a
minute, about 100-fold.

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:18.590
And the ultra-thin fissile
layer requires a finite fission

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:21.840
probability, because of
these many traversals.

00:23:21.840 --> 00:23:24.720
And the new fission produced
neutrons, of course,

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:28.320
will get thrown into the
box, and it will start again.

00:23:28.320 --> 00:23:32.700
More precisely, we
assume the empty cavity

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:36.630
is covered with a layer.

00:23:36.630 --> 00:23:39.360
The configuration is similar
to a black-body radiator.

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:41.590
And I'll remind you, the
black-body radiation in flux

00:23:41.590 --> 00:23:44.070
is uniform independent
of the shape.

00:23:44.070 --> 00:23:45.900
And the flux is
determined essentially

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:48.870
by the properties of the
material, not by the geometry.

00:23:48.870 --> 00:23:51.210
The same thing will
also happen here.

00:23:51.210 --> 00:23:54.450
And identifying independent
shape of the cavity, provided,

00:23:54.450 --> 00:23:56.160
of course, it's well-behaved.

00:23:56.160 --> 00:23:58.530
And therefore it doesn't
matter what the shape is.

00:23:58.530 --> 00:24:00.030
You will get this result.

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:03.210
And also neutron temperature,
and therefore neutron energy,

00:24:03.210 --> 00:24:06.540
will be relayed to the center
by the fuel but the quality

00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:07.750
of the container.

00:24:07.750 --> 00:24:10.415
So temperature will
be fully thermalized

00:24:10.415 --> 00:24:12.040
with the temperature
of your container.

00:24:12.040 --> 00:24:14.290
So you'll know very precisely
what the energy neutrons

00:24:14.290 --> 00:24:15.440
you're dealing with.

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:17.148
And, of course, no
neutron will come out,

00:24:17.148 --> 00:24:18.840
because, being a
black body, it will

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:20.040
contain the neutrons inside.

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:23.160
If they're inside,
they're not outside.

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:26.310
Now, the theory of enhancement
can be easily done.

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:28.060
I will not spend
much time with this.

00:24:28.060 --> 00:24:32.200
It tells you that, if
you have any shape,

00:24:32.200 --> 00:24:36.120
and you take the reflector
away, and then you put it back

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:37.860
and you leave the
same source, and you

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:41.130
ask to change enhancement of the
flux, because of the reflector

00:24:41.130 --> 00:24:45.378
on, that enhancement factor is
equal to that expression which

00:24:45.378 --> 00:24:47.670
is square root of elastic
scattered [INAUDIBLE] capture

00:24:47.670 --> 00:24:49.230
cross-section of scattering.

00:24:49.230 --> 00:24:51.900
Elastic is typical 10 barns.

00:24:51.900 --> 00:24:55.510
In a capture cross cross-section
is order of millibarns.

00:24:55.510 --> 00:24:58.030
So we are talking about a
ratio 10 to the 4, 10 to the 5,

00:24:58.030 --> 00:24:59.375
between the two numbers.

00:24:59.375 --> 00:25:01.390
And therefore, when you
take the square root,

00:25:01.390 --> 00:25:03.550
you get good results.

00:25:03.550 --> 00:25:06.550
And there are two parameters
which are important, here.

00:25:06.550 --> 00:25:10.420
One is the diffusion
length, diffusion length,

00:25:10.420 --> 00:25:14.940
which means the size of
your sphere of containment--

00:25:14.940 --> 00:25:16.860
in other words, the
thickness of your walls

00:25:16.860 --> 00:25:18.980
that you have to
use to do the job.

00:25:18.980 --> 00:25:20.730
And the second one is
enhancement factor--

00:25:20.730 --> 00:25:22.690
by how much you
increase the flux.

00:25:22.690 --> 00:25:26.350
Now, if you get graphite, you
get 27 centimeters diffusion

00:25:26.350 --> 00:25:26.850
length.

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:30.570
And you get 94 enhancement.

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:32.780
Beryllium will do fine, as well.

00:25:32.780 --> 00:25:33.730
It is much smaller.

00:25:33.730 --> 00:25:38.350
It's 12 centimeters, but your
enhancement factor only 73.

00:25:38.350 --> 00:25:42.980
Lithium will be
completely transparent.

00:25:42.980 --> 00:25:45.940
And liquid deuterium will give
an enormous advancement factor,

00:25:45.940 --> 00:25:47.920
but you also have
very long length.

00:25:47.920 --> 00:25:49.540
So you'll have to
build a huge device.

00:25:49.540 --> 00:25:50.740
It's not practical.

00:25:50.740 --> 00:25:53.320
And liquid hydrogen
is also excluded.

00:25:53.320 --> 00:25:55.450
So, essentially,
you are ending up

00:25:55.450 --> 00:25:58.750
with beryllium and
beryllium oxide and carbon

00:25:58.750 --> 00:26:01.210
as interesting materials.

00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:06.390
And then you try to calculate
with this system criticality.

00:26:06.390 --> 00:26:09.130
And you do achieve
criticality rather rapidly.

00:26:09.130 --> 00:26:12.130
Here is the thickness
of the carbon

00:26:12.130 --> 00:26:15.070
wall, which is increased.

00:26:15.070 --> 00:26:16.660
Sorry-- here.

00:26:16.660 --> 00:26:19.600
I increase the thickness
of the carbon wall.

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:21.290
And then, here, I
put the k value--

00:26:21.290 --> 00:26:23.320
which means k equal
1 means criticality,

00:26:23.320 --> 00:26:26.930
means you get back as
many neutrons as you made.

00:26:26.930 --> 00:26:31.300
And if I have a
layer of 1 milligram

00:26:31.300 --> 00:26:33.430
per square centimeter,
1,100 layers,

00:26:33.430 --> 00:26:36.760
you can see I get critical
with about 250 grams

00:26:36.760 --> 00:26:40.140
per square centimeter, which
means 1 meter 20 of carbon.

00:26:40.140 --> 00:26:41.720
It's a bit too much.

00:26:41.720 --> 00:26:43.660
But if I go to 3
milligrams, you see

00:26:43.660 --> 00:26:47.980
I'm already running
at 75 here, grams

00:26:47.980 --> 00:26:51.980
per square centimeter, which
means about 35 centimeters,

00:26:51.980 --> 00:26:53.560
which is that much.

00:26:53.560 --> 00:26:54.770
This is 22 centimeters.

00:26:54.770 --> 00:26:56.870
[INAUDIBLE] flying
over the Atlantic.

00:26:56.870 --> 00:26:59.150
That's [INAUDIBLE]
22 centimeters.

00:26:59.150 --> 00:27:03.670
And so it's a quite reasonably
small amount of reflector.

00:27:03.670 --> 00:27:06.160
And beryllium oxide can
do it fine, even better.

00:27:06.160 --> 00:27:08.500
And you can see that,
indeed, criticality

00:27:08.500 --> 00:27:10.440
is obtained with
layers which are only

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:12.880
a few milligrams per
square centimeter.

00:27:12.880 --> 00:27:16.090
So it is possible,
with this technology,

00:27:16.090 --> 00:27:20.200
to make a critical reactor
which will operate with a very

00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:21.490
small amount of material.

00:27:21.490 --> 00:27:23.200
Remember, 1 milligram
a square centimeter

00:27:23.200 --> 00:27:26.680
is 10 grams per square
meter of surface.

00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:29.080
We are talking about
probably 100 square meters,

00:27:29.080 --> 00:27:30.820
for this mission,
so we're talking

00:27:30.820 --> 00:27:33.460
about between 5 and 10 kilograms
[INAUDIBLE] of americium

00:27:33.460 --> 00:27:35.500
to burn.

00:27:35.500 --> 00:27:36.770
Which is not very much.

00:27:36.770 --> 00:27:42.190
In fact, americium has the same
properties as uranium 230--

00:27:42.190 --> 00:27:46.540
plutonium 238, which is
used now on Cassini mission

00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:48.070
and was on Voyager.

00:27:48.070 --> 00:27:50.820
And usually you had more
than these kilograms on it.

00:27:50.820 --> 00:27:54.140
So it's deja vu, as
one says in French.

00:27:54.140 --> 00:27:56.980
Now, the problem is,
which fuel element you

00:27:56.980 --> 00:27:58.945
like to use very quickly?

00:27:58.945 --> 00:28:01.570
You have to have a high fission
probability, exceptionally long

00:28:01.570 --> 00:28:04.390
lifetime, and
not-too-extravagant production

00:28:04.390 --> 00:28:05.860
procedure.

00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:10.460
Now, of course, americium
242m is an ideal device.

00:28:10.460 --> 00:28:12.310
This is a cross-section.

00:28:12.310 --> 00:28:15.150
Notice the cross-section drops,
when a temperature goes up.

00:28:15.150 --> 00:28:18.850
This is the temperature of your
Hohlraum, 1,000 degrees 2,000

00:28:18.850 --> 00:28:19.630
degrees.

00:28:19.630 --> 00:28:21.380
So you're running here
in this region that

00:28:21.380 --> 00:28:22.620
correspond to those energies.

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:26.560
Capture is very high, fission
very high, capture very small.

00:28:26.560 --> 00:28:28.970
Big ratio between the two.

00:28:28.970 --> 00:28:31.020
Now, if the other element
which is not so bad

00:28:31.020 --> 00:28:35.905
is plutonium 239, which is
well-known as a resonance

00:28:35.905 --> 00:28:39.700
here, at about 0.3,
0.24 electrovolts.

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:41.500
So that could be
used if you want

00:28:41.500 --> 00:28:45.460
to make a container which had
high temperature, for instance

00:28:45.460 --> 00:28:47.380
of the order of 3,000 degrees.

00:28:47.380 --> 00:28:49.490
Then, in this case, you
could also use plutonium.

00:28:49.490 --> 00:28:53.020
But you'll have problem of
temperature coefficient.

00:28:53.020 --> 00:28:56.190
Here you have very nice negative
temperature coefficient,

00:28:56.190 --> 00:28:57.440
because of this cross-section.

00:28:57.440 --> 00:28:59.430
So plutonium is second choice.

00:28:59.430 --> 00:29:02.080
americium is the first choice.

00:29:02.080 --> 00:29:04.388
So I've done a very
complete burn-up

00:29:04.388 --> 00:29:05.680
through a reactor of this type.

00:29:05.680 --> 00:29:08.140
I'm not spending
time to discuss that.

00:29:08.140 --> 00:29:10.330
Here is what happens
when you take

00:29:10.330 --> 00:29:15.280
1.4 kilograms of that
americium and you burn it.

00:29:15.280 --> 00:29:18.050
You can have a
fairly long burnup.

00:29:18.050 --> 00:29:22.930
"Burnup" means megawatt
multiplied by day,

00:29:22.930 --> 00:29:23.560
per kilogram.

00:29:23.560 --> 00:29:25.750
Means one kilogram will
give you that many megawatt

00:29:25.750 --> 00:29:27.520
per that many days.

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:29.020
So 1 kilogram,
here, will give you

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:33.520
400 megawatts for one day,
or 1 megawatt for 400 days,

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:36.132
which is much bigger than what
you get for a normal reactor.

00:29:36.132 --> 00:29:37.840
A normal reactor, you
get a burnup of 20,

00:29:37.840 --> 00:29:39.850
30, in those units.

00:29:39.850 --> 00:29:42.880
Essentially comes from
the fact that the system

00:29:42.880 --> 00:29:46.240
is much more efficient,
when it comes to burning.

00:29:46.240 --> 00:29:49.150
For instance, here,
you can see, during

00:29:49.150 --> 00:29:55.640
this 500-kilogram,
500-megawatt day per kilogram,

00:29:55.640 --> 00:30:00.430
the americium coming down from
1.4 kilograms to about 0.7,

00:30:00.430 --> 00:30:04.460
americium being burnt, americium
243 produced by the captures,

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:06.250
since there's a clean situation.

00:30:06.250 --> 00:30:10.620
And here it stops, because
it gets below critical.

00:30:10.620 --> 00:30:13.870
And so you can get a lot
of energy out of this.

00:30:13.870 --> 00:30:16.040
You can burn about half.

00:30:16.040 --> 00:30:19.270
Even more than half of
your americium can go.

00:30:19.270 --> 00:30:22.090
And 3 milligram per
square centimeter layer

00:30:22.090 --> 00:30:24.680
delivers that much power.

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:27.130
And, of course, if you have
a much longer operation,

00:30:27.130 --> 00:30:29.690
you have to remake your layer.

00:30:29.690 --> 00:30:30.650
It's a very thin layer.

00:30:30.650 --> 00:30:32.460
It can be deposited
in some kind of a way.

00:30:32.460 --> 00:30:34.990
This is-- the whole technology
has to be developed.

00:30:34.990 --> 00:30:38.560
But obviously,
periodic refueling

00:30:38.560 --> 00:30:41.650
may have to be
envisaged, eventually.

00:30:41.650 --> 00:30:46.590
Now, how do you reduce the
americium very rapidly?

00:30:46.590 --> 00:30:50.340
Well, essentially, you
start from americium 241,

00:30:50.340 --> 00:30:53.420
which is naturally grown
in aged reactor-spent fuel.

00:30:53.420 --> 00:30:55.850
Plutonium 241 has a
lifetime of 14 years

00:30:55.850 --> 00:30:57.700
and goes into americium.

00:30:57.700 --> 00:30:59.990
And then you make
a neutron capture.

00:30:59.990 --> 00:31:02.390
And here is to explain how
you do the neutron capture.

00:31:02.390 --> 00:31:04.310
And then you get
an extra neutron,

00:31:04.310 --> 00:31:06.240
and you end up to 242m.

00:31:06.240 --> 00:31:11.250
242-not-m is not stable.

00:31:11.250 --> 00:31:13.910
And so the only thing
left is that one.

00:31:13.910 --> 00:31:17.180
So let's come back to the
question of heating of the gas

00:31:17.180 --> 00:31:18.990
with those fission fragments.

00:31:18.990 --> 00:31:21.500
And so let's assume
we have a tube.

00:31:21.500 --> 00:31:23.400
Typically, we are
talking about a tube

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:27.040
which is 2 and 1/2 meters long,
30 centimeter in diameter.

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:29.690
This is like a fuel
rod in a reactor,

00:31:29.690 --> 00:31:31.820
only a fuel rod in a
reactor is full of material

00:31:31.820 --> 00:31:33.500
and the cooling is outside.

00:31:33.500 --> 00:31:36.290
Here, we are talking about
a fuel rod, which is empty.

00:31:36.290 --> 00:31:38.840
And the fuel, instead of
being deposited as the volume,

00:31:38.840 --> 00:31:41.060
is deposited to the
surface, inside.

00:31:41.060 --> 00:31:44.360
And then we get gas in, and then
the gas go through this thing,

00:31:44.360 --> 00:31:46.040
and it gets heated up.

00:31:46.040 --> 00:31:47.390
Let's see what happens, here.

00:31:47.390 --> 00:31:49.630
You have a tube, 30
centimeters in diameter--

00:31:49.630 --> 00:31:52.170
in this particular
case, 2 meter 50 long.

00:31:52.170 --> 00:31:56.630
There is a thin wall which is
cooled from liquid from outside

00:31:56.630 --> 00:32:00.560
and produces 2 watts per
square centimeter of heat

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:05.130
and produces 3.95
megawatts of power.

00:32:05.130 --> 00:32:09.425
This rod is immersed, as you
will see, in a lithium bath.

00:32:09.425 --> 00:32:11.300
On this wall, there is
americium, 3 milligram

00:32:11.300 --> 00:32:12.217
per square millimeter.

00:32:12.217 --> 00:32:16.640
This total gram is [INAUDIBLE]
71 gram, so it's not very much.

00:32:16.640 --> 00:32:19.400
And there is a little bit of
gas flowing through this system,

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:21.290
1.2 grams per second--

00:32:21.290 --> 00:32:25.040
so, 58 microgram per
square centimeter.

00:32:25.040 --> 00:32:27.290
So it's very slow-flow gas.

00:32:27.290 --> 00:32:30.500
The whole damn thing is taking
1 gram of gas per second.

00:32:30.500 --> 00:32:33.398
And the gas is
flowing very slowly.

00:32:33.398 --> 00:32:34.190
It leaves the wall.

00:32:34.190 --> 00:32:37.160
It departs from the wall,
and it [INAUDIBLE] warms up.

00:32:37.160 --> 00:32:39.243
And when it comes down
here, you will see,

00:32:39.243 --> 00:32:41.660
from exact calculation, there
is temperatures of the order

00:32:41.660 --> 00:32:45.350
of 10,000, 15,000 degrees.

00:32:45.350 --> 00:32:51.750
And, in fact, it's the wall
are cold, and the gas is hot.

00:32:51.750 --> 00:32:52.918
So it's maintained.

00:32:52.918 --> 00:32:54.710
This is my statement
maintained [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:54.710 --> 00:32:59.010
foil reasonable temperature
cooling from outside the wall,

00:32:59.010 --> 00:33:02.060
the sheet, the foil.

00:33:02.060 --> 00:33:04.270
And the gas is
moving from the wall.

00:33:04.270 --> 00:33:06.228
And when it gets
in, it gets hot.

00:33:06.228 --> 00:33:07.770
And, by the time it
comes down there,

00:33:07.770 --> 00:33:10.380
it goes between 10,000 and
15,000 degrees [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:10.380 --> 00:33:13.560
which limits there are
and how can you get there.

00:33:13.560 --> 00:33:15.810
Now, temperature limitation
due to mechanical strength

00:33:15.810 --> 00:33:18.780
of nuclear fuel are removed,
because the fuel is cold

00:33:18.780 --> 00:33:21.690
and the gas is hot.

00:33:21.690 --> 00:33:23.430
Now, there is another--

00:33:23.430 --> 00:33:25.780
there are several gifts
of nature in here,

00:33:25.780 --> 00:33:28.050
which are worth reminding you.

00:33:28.050 --> 00:33:33.650
First of all, the range of
the fission fragments exiting

00:33:33.650 --> 00:33:36.290
from 3 milligram
material has been

00:33:36.290 --> 00:33:39.420
calculated for the actual
Z and A distribution.

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:43.810
And it turns out that
the specific ionization

00:33:43.810 --> 00:33:47.800
per unit of mass is, in
hydrogen, much greater than 1

00:33:47.800 --> 00:33:49.150
in americium.

00:33:49.150 --> 00:33:53.330
There is almost a factor 20
higher specific ionization loss

00:33:53.330 --> 00:33:55.900
in hydrogen, with
respect to americium.

00:33:55.900 --> 00:33:57.152
What's the cost of that?

00:33:57.152 --> 00:33:58.610
The cost of that
is the following--

00:33:58.610 --> 00:34:02.300
that ionization losses only work
when the particle traveling is

00:34:02.300 --> 00:34:05.600
faster than the electron
bound in the atom.

00:34:05.600 --> 00:34:09.659
And the sufficient fragment
is beta equal 0.04, 4%.

00:34:09.659 --> 00:34:14.719
So many of the electrons
bound in the americium foil

00:34:14.719 --> 00:34:21.570
have a speed which is higher
than the speed of the fission

00:34:21.570 --> 00:34:22.070
fragments.

00:34:22.070 --> 00:34:24.500
And therefore they are
essentially not participating

00:34:24.500 --> 00:34:26.600
in the ionization loss.

00:34:26.600 --> 00:34:33.690
If you take hydrogen, well,
the velocities is 1 over alpha,

00:34:33.690 --> 00:34:35.835
and therefore it's much
smaller than the velocity

00:34:35.835 --> 00:34:38.210
of the fission fragment, so
that you have high ionization

00:34:38.210 --> 00:34:39.350
losses.

00:34:39.350 --> 00:34:44.010
You can see here that the range
of these fission fragments

00:34:44.010 --> 00:34:47.929
gives about 10 microns,
as I said, in americium,

00:34:47.929 --> 00:34:51.739
but it's less than
0.5 micron in gas.

00:34:51.739 --> 00:34:55.120
So you get an enhancement
of a factor 20

00:34:55.120 --> 00:34:59.360
in ionization losses in the gas,
which is [INAUDIBLE] extremely

00:34:59.360 --> 00:35:00.730
important.

00:35:00.730 --> 00:35:04.190
And so even 1/2 a milligram
per square centimeter gas

00:35:04.190 --> 00:35:07.910
will absorb the fission
fragment completely.

00:35:07.910 --> 00:35:09.980
So you can operate
very low pressure.

00:35:09.980 --> 00:35:11.820
Actually, in reality,
the fission fragments

00:35:11.820 --> 00:35:14.450
go in all possible direction,
so the range is only

00:35:14.450 --> 00:35:16.970
the upper limit to the travel.

00:35:16.970 --> 00:35:22.070
So let me take two infinite
planes coated with americium,

00:35:22.070 --> 00:35:25.770
defining a volume filled
with hydrogen gas.

00:35:25.770 --> 00:35:28.130
So you have two planes, here.

00:35:28.130 --> 00:35:31.850
And these two planes
are now filled with gas.

00:35:31.850 --> 00:35:34.940
And the fission
fragment traversing it.

00:35:34.940 --> 00:35:38.010
The thickness is 1/2 a
milligram per square centimeter,

00:35:38.010 --> 00:35:40.390
so it's about 20
centimeter, 3 atmospheres,

00:35:40.390 --> 00:35:42.860
just to get
[INAUDIBLE] understood.

00:35:42.860 --> 00:35:44.840
Even at high temperature.

00:35:44.840 --> 00:35:46.820
And then you have,
this is the kind

00:35:46.820 --> 00:35:48.845
of heat produced by
the wall to the left.

00:35:48.845 --> 00:35:50.720
This is the kind of heat
produced by the wall

00:35:50.720 --> 00:35:51.472
to the right.

00:35:51.472 --> 00:35:52.930
And when you put
them together, you

00:35:52.930 --> 00:35:55.370
get a reasonably
flat distribution.

00:35:55.370 --> 00:35:59.730
The important number here is
this very simple one dimension

00:35:59.730 --> 00:36:03.140
of this drawing, which
in fact will be--

00:36:03.140 --> 00:36:06.500
is worse than the case of
the tube, which I'll discuss

00:36:06.500 --> 00:36:08.450
two dimension in a minute.

00:36:08.450 --> 00:36:14.600
You get, with 400 watts per
square centimeter on this foil,

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:18.810
you get about half a
megawatt per gram in the gas.

00:36:18.810 --> 00:36:22.460
Now, a megawatt
is enormous power.

00:36:22.460 --> 00:36:24.170
A megawatt in a
gram is just putting

00:36:24.170 --> 00:36:26.600
a megawatt on a mosquito.

00:36:26.600 --> 00:36:30.920
And obviously, if you put
it a megawatt on a mosquito,

00:36:30.920 --> 00:36:32.750
it will fry very rapidly.

00:36:32.750 --> 00:36:35.300
And therefore the
idea is that you

00:36:35.300 --> 00:36:40.850
can keep the foil under very
normal condition of a reactor

00:36:40.850 --> 00:36:43.040
but get real hell
inside the gas.

00:36:43.040 --> 00:36:45.770
As I said, you can get
a megawatt per gram

00:36:45.770 --> 00:36:50.390
with hundreds of watt per
square centimeter on the wall.

00:36:50.390 --> 00:36:53.960
Now, this whole thing is
played the following way.

00:36:53.960 --> 00:36:59.420
Here you have, in this
curve, here, the watts--

00:36:59.420 --> 00:37:01.310
unfortunately, it's hard to see.

00:37:01.310 --> 00:37:02.550
I cannot even see, myself.

00:37:02.550 --> 00:37:05.050
I wonder whether you can
read, yourself, over there.

00:37:05.050 --> 00:37:09.640
Here you have one megawatt per
gram, 100 kilowatt per gram,

00:37:09.640 --> 00:37:10.988
10 megawatts per gram.

00:37:10.988 --> 00:37:12.530
And here you have
[INAUDIBLE] surface

00:37:12.530 --> 00:37:14.860
power density watts
per square centimeter,

00:37:14.860 --> 00:37:17.018
100 watts per square
centimeter, 400 watts

00:37:17.018 --> 00:37:19.310
per square centimeter, 800
watts per square centimeter.

00:37:19.310 --> 00:37:22.733
This is the kind of region which
normal reactor is operating.

00:37:22.733 --> 00:37:24.650
And you can see that you
can go all the way up

00:37:24.650 --> 00:37:27.950
to about 1 megawatt
per gram, in a gas,

00:37:27.950 --> 00:37:32.420
with reasonable
heating on the walls.

00:37:32.420 --> 00:37:39.080
And that is, of course,
transform into temperature,

00:37:39.080 --> 00:37:43.130
through the enthalpy diagram
for hydrogen, where you have--

00:37:43.130 --> 00:37:46.520
here, you have enthalpy
10 to the 5, 10 to the 6.

00:37:46.520 --> 00:37:48.035
This is in joule per gram.

00:37:48.035 --> 00:37:49.660
And you have temperature
[INAUDIBLE] 10

00:37:49.660 --> 00:37:52.490
to the 3, 10 to the
4, 4 10 to the 4.

00:37:52.490 --> 00:37:55.910
Now, you have to pay a little
attention to this curve.

00:37:55.910 --> 00:38:00.390
Here, up to 3,000 degrees, the
enthalpy is rising linearly.

00:38:00.390 --> 00:38:01.400
This is a perfect gas.

00:38:01.400 --> 00:38:04.400
Enthalpy specific
heat is constant.

00:38:04.400 --> 00:38:09.260
It's essentially a temperature
of a single hydrogen molecules.

00:38:09.260 --> 00:38:13.730
At about 3,000 degrees,
the molecule disassociates,

00:38:13.730 --> 00:38:15.230
becomes [INAUDIBLE].

00:38:15.230 --> 00:38:19.100
Then you get the enthalpy
to do the destruction.

00:38:19.100 --> 00:38:20.695
That is, like, a
chemical reaction.

00:38:20.695 --> 00:38:22.820
You put energy in it;
eventually it will come back,

00:38:22.820 --> 00:38:24.410
after you've done it.

00:38:24.410 --> 00:38:26.130
And then you start
with another slope,

00:38:26.130 --> 00:38:29.690
which is twice this
sharp, because you

00:38:29.690 --> 00:38:31.550
have twice as many atoms there.

00:38:31.550 --> 00:38:35.360
And therefore you have
a higher specific heat

00:38:35.360 --> 00:38:37.220
contained for a given gas.

00:38:37.220 --> 00:38:39.420
And then you get to
about 10,000 degrees.

00:38:39.420 --> 00:38:43.490
And when you get to 10,000
degrees, the gas gets ionized.

00:38:43.490 --> 00:38:47.480
And then you are getting the
13 electrovolts of ionization.

00:38:47.480 --> 00:38:51.380
Remember, 10,000 degrees
kelvin is one electrovolt,

00:38:51.380 --> 00:38:53.420
but here we are
talking about having

00:38:53.420 --> 00:38:58.770
recovering something like of
the order of 10 electrovolts.

00:38:58.770 --> 00:39:01.340
And therefore the enthalpy
grows tremendously,

00:39:01.340 --> 00:39:04.310
even if the temperature
doesn't go very high.

00:39:04.310 --> 00:39:06.710
And essentially,
you are now talking

00:39:06.710 --> 00:39:09.180
about enthalpy,
joule over gram, is

00:39:09.180 --> 00:39:12.700
going to power watts per
gram times dwell time,

00:39:12.700 --> 00:39:14.900
time which you are
in the exposure,

00:39:14.900 --> 00:39:16.850
time gas takes to come
out of the container.

00:39:16.850 --> 00:39:19.970
Typically, we're talking
about of several seconds,

00:39:19.970 --> 00:39:21.530
in this machine.

00:39:21.530 --> 00:39:26.270
So we are talking about seconds,
multiply megawatt per gram,

00:39:26.270 --> 00:39:28.040
equal to gigajoule per gram.

00:39:28.040 --> 00:39:29.640
Gigajoules per
gram is right here.

00:39:29.640 --> 00:39:31.730
So we're talking about
temperatures 10,000, 15,000

00:39:31.730 --> 00:39:33.260
degrees.

00:39:33.260 --> 00:39:37.880
And, when Nerva, you have
fast motion, short dwell,

00:39:37.880 --> 00:39:40.090
here you have slow
motion, long dwell.

00:39:40.090 --> 00:39:42.320
And this gives you
the impulse which you

00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:44.230
can get with 10,000 degrees.

00:39:44.230 --> 00:39:48.200
You have about 25 kilometers
a second exhaust speed

00:39:48.200 --> 00:39:52.960
and about 3,000 in
specific impulse.

00:39:52.960 --> 00:39:54.565
How much time do I have left?

00:39:54.565 --> 00:39:55.838
MODERATOR: So 20 minutes.

00:39:55.838 --> 00:39:56.630
RUBBIA: 20 minutes.

00:39:56.630 --> 00:39:57.740
Okay, fine.

00:39:57.740 --> 00:40:01.700
Now, then, to do
that more clearly,

00:40:01.700 --> 00:40:03.940
we are talking about
parameters of this tube,

00:40:03.940 --> 00:40:07.700
20-centimeter radius, 2
and 1/2 meters in length.

00:40:07.700 --> 00:40:10.810
And we are talking about
1,000 degrees for the wall.

00:40:10.810 --> 00:40:14.290
Final temperature in the
gas of 10,000 degrees.

00:40:14.290 --> 00:40:17.280
And we are talking a very small
amount of gas coming through.

00:40:17.280 --> 00:40:20.260
We are talking about
essentially speed

00:40:20.260 --> 00:40:22.450
of the gas of 0.75
centimeter per second

00:40:22.450 --> 00:40:25.820
in the beginning, 3 from 5 grams
per second through the system.

00:40:25.820 --> 00:40:28.750
And this is the heating stage.

00:40:28.750 --> 00:40:31.330
Then you have an expansion
cone, which brings you down

00:40:31.330 --> 00:40:36.990
to about 25,000, 2,500
in specific impulse,

00:40:36.990 --> 00:40:41.455
so 25,000 meter per second, and
with a thermodynamic efficiency

00:40:41.455 --> 00:40:42.220
of about 17%.

00:40:44.890 --> 00:40:46.780
Now, there is one
technical point

00:40:46.780 --> 00:40:49.610
which I'd quickly
like to mention,

00:40:49.610 --> 00:40:52.330
which is a substantial
limitation to this method,

00:40:52.330 --> 00:40:56.170
to this system, which
are the radiative losses

00:40:56.170 --> 00:40:59.080
of the gas which gets heated.

00:40:59.080 --> 00:41:01.890
When that gas gets very
hot, it starts to radiate--

00:41:01.890 --> 00:41:03.730
emits power away.

00:41:03.730 --> 00:41:05.380
Eventually, if
the power radiated

00:41:05.380 --> 00:41:09.670
is more than the fission power
coming in, you are in trouble.

00:41:09.670 --> 00:41:12.780
Essentially, it's a lot of
work to calculate all this.

00:41:12.780 --> 00:41:16.070
There are type of losses--
line, band, and continuum.

00:41:16.070 --> 00:41:17.710
Now, remember,
[INAUDIBLE] we only

00:41:17.710 --> 00:41:22.000
have atomic hydrogen.
Therefore, the molecular levels

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:23.220
are not there.

00:41:23.220 --> 00:41:26.350
And essentially you're talking
about theoretical hydrogen

00:41:26.350 --> 00:41:30.352
[INAUDIBLE] 1, 2
electrons [INAUDIBLE]..

00:41:30.352 --> 00:41:32.560
[INAUDIBLE] then [INAUDIBLE]
we know all the numbers,

00:41:32.560 --> 00:41:35.740
so all the calculation can
be done very precisely.

00:41:35.740 --> 00:41:39.970
And it turns out that
the radiated power there

00:41:39.970 --> 00:41:44.350
is essentially, the line
emission is quickly absorbed.

00:41:44.350 --> 00:41:50.260
Essentially, the line looks
black because of the absorption

00:41:50.260 --> 00:41:53.560
is, in fact, quite
strongly in the line.

00:41:53.560 --> 00:41:56.740
And therefore, you don't have--

00:41:56.740 --> 00:41:58.120
Essentially, it's like the sun.

00:41:58.120 --> 00:41:59.110
When you look at
the sun, you see

00:41:59.110 --> 00:42:00.130
the sun, the plasma of the sun.

00:42:00.130 --> 00:42:01.180
You see the lines dark.

00:42:01.180 --> 00:42:02.888
You don't see the
light coming out of it.

00:42:02.888 --> 00:42:05.290
So there is no radiation
from the lines.

00:42:05.290 --> 00:42:11.350
There is, however, a substantial
band emission, which is there.

00:42:11.350 --> 00:42:12.490
It has been all calculated.

00:42:12.490 --> 00:42:14.770
I'm not going to get
into details with this.

00:42:14.770 --> 00:42:16.810
Essentially it turns
out that what happens

00:42:16.810 --> 00:42:20.400
is that the light is
growing with the temperature

00:42:20.400 --> 00:42:21.530
of the gas.

00:42:21.530 --> 00:42:24.400
And this is frequency,
and this is the power,

00:42:24.400 --> 00:42:27.300
after a certain finite distance.

00:42:27.300 --> 00:42:30.700
And it's becoming
a lamp, a big lamp.

00:42:30.700 --> 00:42:34.060
And here is the
surviving radiated power,

00:42:34.060 --> 00:42:36.550
as a function of the
distance of the gas.

00:42:36.550 --> 00:42:39.730
This is about 1 centimeter
of gas at 3 atmosphere.

00:42:39.730 --> 00:42:42.100
So most of the light
emitted by the band

00:42:42.100 --> 00:42:45.600
is absorbed immediately in
the region where it's emitted.

00:42:45.600 --> 00:42:49.360
And therefore it contributes
only to heat conductivity.

00:42:49.360 --> 00:42:50.950
But there is a
[INAUDIBLE] component,

00:42:50.950 --> 00:42:53.230
which is this flat
line here, which

00:42:53.230 --> 00:42:57.025
is, in fact, having
a very constant,

00:42:57.025 --> 00:42:59.200
a very long attenuation path.

00:42:59.200 --> 00:43:02.050
And this is the light you can
see on the walls, yourself.

00:43:02.050 --> 00:43:04.720
This is all taken into
account in the calculation.

00:43:04.720 --> 00:43:09.990
And essentially it tells
you that, up to about 10,000

00:43:09.990 --> 00:43:12.550
or 12,000 degrees,
you can operate,

00:43:12.550 --> 00:43:15.310
and above that you have a
problem of radiation which

00:43:15.310 --> 00:43:16.060
you have to solve.

00:43:18.780 --> 00:43:20.340
The other question
which is important

00:43:20.340 --> 00:43:24.160
is the recombination
of the hydrogen.

00:43:24.160 --> 00:43:26.193
The hydrogen is starting
from being-- well,

00:43:26.193 --> 00:43:27.610
let me show you,
here, the curves,

00:43:27.610 --> 00:43:30.060
which is probably the
best way of showing it.

00:43:30.060 --> 00:43:33.500
The hydrogen starts
as a molecule.

00:43:33.500 --> 00:43:37.120
This is this line, here,
is function of temperature.

00:43:37.120 --> 00:43:38.330
This is logarithmic scale.

00:43:38.330 --> 00:43:40.960
Very quickly becomes an atom.

00:43:40.960 --> 00:43:45.652
And then, at about 10,000
degrees, it starts to ionize.

00:43:45.652 --> 00:43:47.110
Here, for instance--
3 atmospheres.

00:43:47.110 --> 00:43:49.080
Here 10,000 degrees
[INAUDIBLE] ionized.

00:43:49.080 --> 00:43:52.700
And at 15,000 degrees,
it's about 50% ionized.

00:43:52.700 --> 00:43:55.030
So essentially what
happens is that you

00:43:55.030 --> 00:43:57.160
are dominated with atomic gas.

00:43:57.160 --> 00:43:58.690
And above a certain
value, you're

00:43:58.690 --> 00:44:04.720
also dominated by plasma which
is substantially ionized.

00:44:04.720 --> 00:44:09.122
The plasma recombination
electron proton--

00:44:09.122 --> 00:44:11.080
in other words, the
combination of the plasma--

00:44:11.080 --> 00:44:12.940
it's a very fast
reaction, because it's

00:44:12.940 --> 00:44:17.500
a gamma plus neutral hydrogen
equal to electron plus proton.

00:44:17.500 --> 00:44:20.140
It's a perfectly
large cross-section.

00:44:20.140 --> 00:44:24.760
The recombination molecule
to atom, it's very slow.

00:44:24.760 --> 00:44:27.430
Remember, two atoms
cannot make a molecule,

00:44:27.430 --> 00:44:29.930
because there is no momentum
energy conservation.

00:44:29.930 --> 00:44:32.177
So a single collision
of two atoms

00:44:32.177 --> 00:44:33.760
will not make a
molecule, because they

00:44:33.760 --> 00:44:36.350
have to emit a gamma, and
the gamma is not there,

00:44:36.350 --> 00:44:37.690
at those temperatures.

00:44:37.690 --> 00:44:40.260
And therefore the
thing is metastable.

00:44:40.260 --> 00:44:45.730
You remember the so-called
hydrogen torch, plasma torch,

00:44:45.730 --> 00:44:49.390
in which you take atomic
molecular hydrogen

00:44:49.390 --> 00:44:51.940
and you make it atomic
hydrogen, and then you

00:44:51.940 --> 00:44:53.590
throw it on a wall.

00:44:53.590 --> 00:44:55.840
And when it hits the wall,
it becomes molecular again,

00:44:55.840 --> 00:44:58.030
because it finds recoil.

00:44:58.030 --> 00:44:59.860
And therefore, it
gives you heat.

00:44:59.860 --> 00:45:02.410
And, in fact, you can melt
a lot of stuff in this way.

00:45:02.410 --> 00:45:06.360
Now, that is essentially what's
happening in our case, here.

00:45:06.360 --> 00:45:09.820
However, as I showed at the
beginning, first of all,

00:45:09.820 --> 00:45:12.910
the gas is essentially--
therefore, our gas is frozen.

00:45:12.910 --> 00:45:15.910
In other words, we expect this
gas to come out essentially

00:45:15.910 --> 00:45:17.800
as an atomic gas.

00:45:17.800 --> 00:45:23.317
However, atomic gas means the
A is 1 instead of being 2.

00:45:23.317 --> 00:45:25.150
And therefore you pick
up a square root of 2

00:45:25.150 --> 00:45:28.880
in the number of the
speed of your gas.

00:45:28.880 --> 00:45:31.690
And therefore, although you
will lose part of the energy

00:45:31.690 --> 00:45:34.148
because you're dealing with an
atomic system rather than it

00:45:34.148 --> 00:45:36.940
being a molecular system,
the effect on the specific

00:45:36.940 --> 00:45:38.960
impulse-- as I showed in
the first transparency,

00:45:38.960 --> 00:45:42.040
which I hope I can find
again, here-- is quite small,

00:45:42.040 --> 00:45:47.930
essentially because of the fact
that the gas is reducing its A.

00:45:47.930 --> 00:45:49.120
And so we have essentially--

00:45:49.120 --> 00:45:52.170
This gas comes out very cold.

00:45:52.170 --> 00:45:58.120
When it hit-- so the
problem of the funnel

00:45:58.120 --> 00:46:00.320
is not a very important point.

00:46:00.320 --> 00:46:02.040
Now, since we are
in computer science,

00:46:02.040 --> 00:46:05.260
here, let me tell you
some computer simulation.

00:46:05.260 --> 00:46:07.550
So I'll feel a little
bit more at home.

00:46:07.550 --> 00:46:10.840
So what we've done-- we have
developed a lot of programs

00:46:10.840 --> 00:46:11.890
which do the following.

00:46:11.890 --> 00:46:14.590
This is doing
fluid-dynamics calculation.

00:46:14.590 --> 00:46:16.900
This is making a
fission-fragment transport heat

00:46:16.900 --> 00:46:17.730
release.

00:46:17.730 --> 00:46:20.920
This is worrying about
radiative effects of the gas.

00:46:20.920 --> 00:46:24.640
And this guy, here, is combining
the energy losses and energy

00:46:24.640 --> 00:46:30.010
radiation to close the loop
with the program, which is

00:46:30.010 --> 00:46:33.910
the program on fluid dynamics.

00:46:33.910 --> 00:46:36.130
So this is a fluid
dynamics program.

00:46:36.130 --> 00:46:40.090
Then it goes into a heating
from fission fragment

00:46:40.090 --> 00:46:42.670
[INAUDIBLE] neutronics
providing the supply.

00:46:42.670 --> 00:46:45.343
Radiation, you lose
power, you make power,

00:46:45.343 --> 00:46:47.260
you take a power balance,
and you put it back,

00:46:47.260 --> 00:46:51.290
and you make [INAUDIBLE]
current calculations.

00:46:51.290 --> 00:46:54.680
And then we have done
several models, here,

00:46:54.680 --> 00:46:59.830
with a typical 20-centimeter,
2 and 2/2 meters machine--

00:46:59.830 --> 00:47:03.500
simulation, rather
sophisticated.

00:47:03.500 --> 00:47:08.050
We have hydrogen inflow
at 100 kelvin, which is

00:47:08.050 --> 00:47:11.710
in fact uniformly distributed.

00:47:11.710 --> 00:47:15.760
And-- this thing
is really horrible.

00:47:15.760 --> 00:47:21.040
And then you get essentially
various intensity of flow.

00:47:21.040 --> 00:47:22.300
Let me show you some results.

00:47:24.930 --> 00:47:30.820
Here is, we are increasing
the temperature of the heater.

00:47:30.820 --> 00:47:33.405
This is half of the cylinder.

00:47:33.405 --> 00:47:36.030
You can see that there's another
half coming on the other side.

00:47:36.030 --> 00:47:38.040
So you start from
the wall, here.

00:47:38.040 --> 00:47:42.810
When you move on, you
heat up the gas slowly,

00:47:42.810 --> 00:47:44.260
until the gas gets very hot.

00:47:44.260 --> 00:47:47.430
And then, eventually, down
here, the gas is the hottest.

00:47:47.430 --> 00:47:50.340
And it comes out
from the funnel.

00:47:50.340 --> 00:47:52.420
You see the same thing
here, case number 3,

00:47:52.420 --> 00:47:55.260
which is the one we consider
the most appropriate.

00:47:55.260 --> 00:47:58.140
And you do get about
10,000 degrees out of that.

00:47:58.140 --> 00:48:02.850
And you do get essentially
all the gas is nicely hot,

00:48:02.850 --> 00:48:04.860
at the end.

00:48:04.860 --> 00:48:08.940
So the fluid dynamics works
extremely well in this story.

00:48:08.940 --> 00:48:12.940
Actually, you can see,
here, global power, power,

00:48:12.940 --> 00:48:14.650
the position and temperatures.

00:48:14.650 --> 00:48:17.880
And you can see, in fact, that
these kind of calculations

00:48:17.880 --> 00:48:19.600
are perfectly adequate.

00:48:19.600 --> 00:48:23.490
So we have a good
fluid-dynamic model of this.

00:48:23.490 --> 00:48:29.730
The heating process of the
fission fragment in the two

00:48:29.730 --> 00:48:31.250
dimensions is quite remarkable.

00:48:31.250 --> 00:48:34.560
Here, we had 200 watts per
square centimeter on the tube.

00:48:34.560 --> 00:48:39.420
And here, we are collecting,
here, 900 watts per milligram.

00:48:39.420 --> 00:48:41.510
So we are collecting
900 kilowatts--

00:48:41.510 --> 00:48:46.250
so, 1 megawatt per
gram of the gas.

00:48:46.250 --> 00:48:49.560
And you see here is the
radius of the container.

00:48:49.560 --> 00:48:51.190
In the central part,
it's very uniform.

00:48:51.190 --> 00:48:54.600
These two lines correspond
to the top and the bottom,

00:48:54.600 --> 00:48:58.290
where there is no coverage,
on one hand, because you're

00:48:58.290 --> 00:49:01.590
going out into the
nozzle, the top,

00:49:01.590 --> 00:49:05.360
because there is
nothing on the top.

00:49:05.360 --> 00:49:08.490
The americium is only the
positive of the sides.

00:49:08.490 --> 00:49:10.080
If you were to
increase the pressure

00:49:10.080 --> 00:49:12.780
from 2 atmospheres
to 12 atmospheres,

00:49:12.780 --> 00:49:14.550
then you will observe this.

00:49:14.550 --> 00:49:16.440
Your fission fragment
would just stop

00:49:16.440 --> 00:49:20.430
before they get to the center,
and only the sides is heated.

00:49:20.430 --> 00:49:21.600
A few more minutes left.

00:49:21.600 --> 00:49:24.245
I can describe you, here, some
of the design consideration.

00:49:24.245 --> 00:49:25.620
As I was saying,
this is the kind

00:49:25.620 --> 00:49:27.660
of tubes we are talking about.

00:49:27.660 --> 00:49:32.540
And then these many tubes
are inserted inside a box.

00:49:32.540 --> 00:49:33.470
So let's look at this.

00:49:33.470 --> 00:49:36.170
This is a realistic engine.

00:49:36.170 --> 00:49:39.050
This will carry
emission, according

00:49:39.050 --> 00:49:43.470
to what [INAUDIBLE] calculating,
over to Mars and back again,

00:49:43.470 --> 00:49:46.430
in the way he was
describing it before.

00:49:46.430 --> 00:49:49.310
It's made essentially
by a series of tubes,

00:49:49.310 --> 00:49:51.230
2 and 1/2 meters long.

00:49:51.230 --> 00:49:53.780
Those tubes are equivalent
to the fuel elements.

00:49:53.780 --> 00:49:56.570
They have, on the bottom,
the nozzle, and therefore

00:49:56.570 --> 00:50:00.280
they can be clamped on
a baseline, if you like.

00:50:00.280 --> 00:50:05.300
They are immersed inside a
sea of lithium, liquid lithium

00:50:05.300 --> 00:50:07.940
bath, which is the
coolant And these

00:50:07.940 --> 00:50:12.770
works as a boiling-lithium
reactor, if you like.

00:50:12.770 --> 00:50:15.080
The lithium is all around here.

00:50:15.080 --> 00:50:18.410
And it touches the
outside of those tubes,

00:50:18.410 --> 00:50:22.520
so that the outside wall is
cooled with this lithium.

00:50:22.520 --> 00:50:25.880
And the lithium is
traveling all over, here.

00:50:25.880 --> 00:50:30.590
There are control bars
coming into the Hohlraum,

00:50:30.590 --> 00:50:36.200
so you take away the neutrons
to control the process.

00:50:36.200 --> 00:50:42.480
The system is essentially
a lithium-cooled system.

00:50:42.480 --> 00:50:44.900
You have lithium which boils.

00:50:44.900 --> 00:50:47.210
Lithium becomes vapor.

00:50:47.210 --> 00:50:50.400
The vapor lithium goes
over to the panels,

00:50:50.400 --> 00:50:52.220
which are the cooling panels.

00:50:52.220 --> 00:50:53.940
There it condenses again.

00:50:53.940 --> 00:50:57.610
It becomes liquid and then is
recollected and put back again.

00:50:57.610 --> 00:50:59.940
And so it operates at
constant temperature.

00:50:59.940 --> 00:51:02.395
But there is the heating--

00:51:02.395 --> 00:51:03.770
the fraction of
the heat which is

00:51:03.770 --> 00:51:08.150
used by the boiled-off heat,
which is used for cooling.

00:51:08.150 --> 00:51:09.700
These are the numbers
indicated here.

00:51:09.700 --> 00:51:14.180
These we are talking about
thrust power of 30 megawatt.

00:51:14.180 --> 00:51:16.940
Overall thrust efficient is 17%.

00:51:16.940 --> 00:51:20.340
177 megawatts of
consumption, total.

00:51:20.340 --> 00:51:24.960
Exhaust speed 35 kilometers
per hour, per second.

00:51:24.960 --> 00:51:30.180
Propellant flow is 50 grams
of hydrogen per second.

00:51:30.180 --> 00:51:33.360
The thrust is about
2,000 newtons.

00:51:33.360 --> 00:51:36.880
The foil power density is 2
watts per square centimeters.

00:51:36.880 --> 00:51:41.530
There are 88 square
meters of americium.

00:51:41.530 --> 00:51:45.770
And this machine will run with
2.4 kilograms of americium--

00:51:45.770 --> 00:51:47.270
so, very little.

00:51:47.270 --> 00:51:49.103
And the refuel time
will be every nine days.

00:51:49.103 --> 00:51:50.937
Every nine days, you
go in there [INAUDIBLE]

00:51:50.937 --> 00:51:53.140
you just put your tubes
or whatever, to get going.

00:51:53.140 --> 00:51:55.450
So we're talking about a
few kilograms of material.

00:51:55.450 --> 00:51:56.580
And you can get nine days.

00:51:56.580 --> 00:51:58.205
Over nine days, you
can get this power.

00:51:58.205 --> 00:52:01.090
This is sufficient to
[INAUDIBLE] the mission.

00:52:01.090 --> 00:52:03.460
I mean, you will
do these things,

00:52:03.460 --> 00:52:05.590
refueling not during the
mission-- in the periods

00:52:05.590 --> 00:52:06.850
in which you are recovering.

00:52:06.850 --> 00:52:08.140
So it's quantized
in a proper way.

00:52:08.140 --> 00:52:09.640
The other way of
doing it that would

00:52:09.640 --> 00:52:12.500
be to do a tube-share
type of thing.

00:52:12.500 --> 00:52:15.420
In other words, each tube
is blowing separately.

00:52:15.420 --> 00:52:19.360
But that is a question
of the technical design.

00:52:19.360 --> 00:52:21.410
Now, obviously, this is an idea.

00:52:21.410 --> 00:52:23.990
As I said, all ideas have
a chance not to work.

00:52:23.990 --> 00:52:26.600
This has certainly got a
high probability not to work.

00:52:26.600 --> 00:52:29.540
But it's still fun,
worrying about.

00:52:29.540 --> 00:52:32.050
First of all, the
behavior of the americium

00:52:32.050 --> 00:52:35.600
under extensive exposure
to hydrogen is the problem.

00:52:35.600 --> 00:52:41.420
The good news is that the
Nerva engine also has hydrogen

00:52:41.420 --> 00:52:43.790
and also has a fuel.

00:52:43.790 --> 00:52:45.260
It's not americium,
it's uranium,

00:52:45.260 --> 00:52:47.330
but they're very
similar chemically.

00:52:47.330 --> 00:52:50.000
Which is in contact with
hydrogen, at high temperature,

00:52:50.000 --> 00:52:52.380
and it doesn't go away.

00:52:52.380 --> 00:52:54.440
The bad news, of course,
is the position is thin.

00:52:54.440 --> 00:52:58.880
In the case of Nerva, you've
got a piece of solid rock.

00:52:58.880 --> 00:53:01.150
The other question is
radiative loss of the hot gas.

00:53:01.150 --> 00:53:03.750
There's still some work to
do, experimental work to do.

00:53:03.750 --> 00:53:06.000
Below 10,000 degrees, there
is essentially very little

00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:07.160
or no radiation.

00:53:07.160 --> 00:53:08.830
If you go [INAUDIBLE]
above that,

00:53:08.830 --> 00:53:11.372
the calculation are one thing,
the experiments are the other.

00:53:11.372 --> 00:53:13.020
You've got to do
something about that.

00:53:13.020 --> 00:53:15.110
Now, hydrodynamic
calculation obviously

00:53:15.110 --> 00:53:16.970
is the more important point.

00:53:16.970 --> 00:53:19.880
And there we have questions
like transformation

00:53:19.880 --> 00:53:22.325
in the nozzle from plasma
to the atom and the atom

00:53:22.325 --> 00:53:23.037
to the molecule.

00:53:23.037 --> 00:53:25.370
When it cools down, you have
to understand what it does.

00:53:25.370 --> 00:53:27.530
It's frozen flow, et cetera.

00:53:27.530 --> 00:53:30.430
And also other
possibilities are, instead

00:53:30.430 --> 00:53:32.630
of using hydrogen you could
use any other material.

00:53:32.630 --> 00:53:34.130
And one interesting
material to use,

00:53:34.130 --> 00:53:37.280
it would be lithium hydrate.

00:53:37.280 --> 00:53:39.710
Lithium hydrate is
a material which

00:53:39.710 --> 00:53:44.930
is solid at 650 degrees,
boils, becomes liquid,

00:53:44.930 --> 00:53:48.110
and then you can throw
it into the system.

00:53:48.110 --> 00:53:51.878
It looks like a solid fuel
instead of being a liquid fuel,

00:53:51.878 --> 00:53:53.170
from a practical point of view.

00:53:53.170 --> 00:53:55.220
So you get less
thrust, but you do

00:53:55.220 --> 00:53:56.720
have the advantage
not to go around

00:53:56.720 --> 00:54:00.120
with enormous [INAUDIBLE].

00:54:00.120 --> 00:54:03.840
And, eventually, in a
5- to 10-years period,

00:54:03.840 --> 00:54:06.190
we'll have to do some testing.

00:54:06.190 --> 00:54:08.370
So we are talking about
a test facility which

00:54:08.370 --> 00:54:13.060
should be large enough to test
fully [INAUDIBLE] final tubes.

00:54:13.060 --> 00:54:16.390
Now, in Italy, and in
particular also in America,

00:54:16.390 --> 00:54:18.450
everywhere, every time
you say "a reactor,"

00:54:18.450 --> 00:54:21.620
everybody gets
really up in arms,

00:54:21.620 --> 00:54:24.120
says, my god, you don't want
to do it, et cetera, et cetera.

00:54:24.120 --> 00:54:27.110
So we decided that
we could also do it

00:54:27.110 --> 00:54:29.790
with a subcritical facility.

00:54:29.790 --> 00:54:30.750
It can be done.

00:54:30.750 --> 00:54:34.260
The whole round can be
fed with neutrons, also

00:54:34.260 --> 00:54:37.880
from an accelerator.

00:54:37.880 --> 00:54:39.630
And this type of thing
will allow you also

00:54:39.630 --> 00:54:42.450
to produce americium,
after that.

00:54:42.450 --> 00:54:46.260
And essentially it would allow
to us to get a faster track.

00:54:46.260 --> 00:54:48.592
And the idea is to take
one of these tubes--

00:54:48.592 --> 00:54:50.300
as I was indicating
you, 2 and 1/2 meters

00:54:50.300 --> 00:54:52.980
long, and
40-centimeter diameter.

00:54:52.980 --> 00:54:57.990
And they will give you
about a megawatt of power.

00:54:57.990 --> 00:55:01.710
And exhaust speed will be
35 kilometers per second.

00:55:01.710 --> 00:55:03.660
And you can put that in
the whole round, which

00:55:03.660 --> 00:55:07.410
can be made, for instance,
with [INAUDIBLE] D2O.

00:55:07.410 --> 00:55:10.170
Initially, eventually,
then you can go to lithium.

00:55:10.170 --> 00:55:13.410
And the supplying
neutron will be

00:55:13.410 --> 00:55:15.510
provided by an accelerator
which will give you

00:55:15.510 --> 00:55:17.130
some neutrons in there.

00:55:17.130 --> 00:55:19.380
And then you blow this
horn in this tube,

00:55:19.380 --> 00:55:20.770
and then you collect the gas.

00:55:20.770 --> 00:55:25.380
There's not much gas--
only 1.32 grams per second.

00:55:25.380 --> 00:55:30.420
So you can pump it, you can
freeze it, and you can recycle.

00:55:30.420 --> 00:55:32.700
So that could be a model,
taking one of these tubes

00:55:32.700 --> 00:55:33.408
and make it work.

00:55:33.408 --> 00:55:35.367
And then, if you understand
one of those tubes,

00:55:35.367 --> 00:55:36.150
you could do more.

00:55:36.150 --> 00:55:38.910
Now obviously, we were
talking about engines

00:55:38.910 --> 00:55:41.718
capable of doing manned
mission, but obviously you

00:55:41.718 --> 00:55:42.760
don't to have to do that.

00:55:42.760 --> 00:55:45.180
You can use one tube
of smaller devices

00:55:45.180 --> 00:55:46.982
to carry also
instrumented missions.

00:55:46.982 --> 00:55:49.440
For instance, there are lots
of instrumented missions which

00:55:49.440 --> 00:55:54.390
you could do with 10,
20 tons of initial load,

00:55:54.390 --> 00:55:56.820
rather than using the
100, 150 tons which

00:55:56.820 --> 00:56:00.870
are required to get on
to with a man on Mars.

00:56:00.870 --> 00:56:02.940
And there is a lot kind
of interesting things,

00:56:02.940 --> 00:56:06.390
like the story about Europa,
and so forth and so on.

00:56:06.390 --> 00:56:09.340
I mean, in other words, get into
this thing with these devices.

00:56:09.340 --> 00:56:11.580
Remember this thing
is very modest, here.

00:56:11.580 --> 00:56:14.100
You only have 100 grams--

00:56:14.100 --> 00:56:17.670
this device, 80
grams of americium

00:56:17.670 --> 00:56:21.670
and 1.4 grams per second
of gas, but still gives you

00:56:21.670 --> 00:56:23.220
about a megawatt
of thrust power.

00:56:23.220 --> 00:56:26.190
So it's quite a device.

00:56:26.190 --> 00:56:28.710
You don't have to go
to a gigantic device,

00:56:28.710 --> 00:56:34.410
with a Superman getting
there, to start.

00:56:34.410 --> 00:56:38.700
A thing like that is certainly
already interesting enough

00:56:38.700 --> 00:56:39.400
for a device.

00:56:39.400 --> 00:56:40.650
It's extremely simple.

00:56:40.650 --> 00:56:43.860
There no moving parts.

00:56:43.860 --> 00:56:46.410
There are no high pressures.

00:56:46.410 --> 00:56:50.010
It's essentially a leaky tube
in which you put a little gas

00:56:50.010 --> 00:56:52.470
and you get your fission
fragments into it.

00:56:52.470 --> 00:56:55.713
So it looks like quite
cute thing to try.

00:56:55.713 --> 00:56:56.880
Well, I'll finish with that.

00:56:56.880 --> 00:56:57.669
Thank you.

00:56:57.669 --> 00:57:05.004
[APPLAUSE]

00:57:05.004 --> 00:57:06.960
MODERATOR: We have
time for questions.

00:57:11.361 --> 00:57:16.740
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:57:16.740 --> 00:57:17.480
RUBBIA: Not much.

00:57:17.480 --> 00:57:20.003
The power going to the wall
is below 10,000 degrees.

00:57:20.003 --> 00:57:20.670
It's very small.

00:57:20.670 --> 00:57:22.650
AUDIENCE: And that includes
the radiator [INAUDIBLE]..

00:57:22.650 --> 00:57:24.108
RUBBIA: Oh,
definitely, definitely.

00:57:24.108 --> 00:57:25.500
It's all included.

00:57:25.500 --> 00:57:28.150
I have a curve, here.

00:57:28.150 --> 00:57:31.170
You see, I have a
curve, here, Arthur.

00:57:31.170 --> 00:57:37.240
Here, we have a heating
power of 1.5 megawatts.

00:57:37.240 --> 00:57:42.140
And then this is the power
going onto the walls,

00:57:42.140 --> 00:57:45.550
integrated power
watts, and on the tube.

00:57:45.550 --> 00:57:48.250
And you see that, if you
go to 10,000 degrees,

00:57:48.250 --> 00:57:50.570
you're about 40 kilowatts.

00:57:50.570 --> 00:57:54.800
So you get 40 kilo-- it's
a lamp of 40 kilowatts.

00:57:54.800 --> 00:57:56.585
But the power of
the fission fragment

00:57:56.585 --> 00:57:58.915
is 1 and 1/2 megawatts.

00:57:58.915 --> 00:58:00.540
In fact, the amount
of power coming out

00:58:00.540 --> 00:58:03.320
is only core-- only
[INAUDIBLE] regions.

00:58:03.320 --> 00:58:07.380
See, it rises extremely
rapidly with temperature.

00:58:07.380 --> 00:58:09.960
At 8,000 degrees,
there is nothing.

00:58:09.960 --> 00:58:12.690
So only the last
little bit is emittive.

00:58:12.690 --> 00:58:17.100
So you will see a very luminous
core, starting 8,000 degrees

00:58:17.100 --> 00:58:18.300
and going up to 10,000.

00:58:18.300 --> 00:58:23.190
That region, there,
will be quite a light.

00:58:23.190 --> 00:58:25.620
But that volume is a small
fraction of the total,

00:58:25.620 --> 00:58:28.612
because most of the
work which you do

00:58:28.612 --> 00:58:29.820
you do at lower temperatures.

00:58:29.820 --> 00:58:33.350
Because there you
pay the enthalpy

00:58:33.350 --> 00:58:38.130
of separating the atom,
the molecule to the atom.

00:58:38.130 --> 00:58:40.980
And that is where
most of the work is.

00:58:40.980 --> 00:58:43.710
And therefore, there is
relatively little gas

00:58:43.710 --> 00:58:45.960
which is very hot in it.

00:58:45.960 --> 00:58:47.440
And that justifies this thing.

00:58:47.440 --> 00:58:50.160
But this is a perfectly
legitimate and exact

00:58:50.160 --> 00:58:53.420
calculation, using the correct
cross-section for hydrogen

00:58:53.420 --> 00:58:55.590
and atomic modes, and
et cetera, et cetera,

00:58:55.590 --> 00:58:58.950
with a complete
simulation of hydro,

00:58:58.950 --> 00:59:02.220
et cetera, including all
kind of sophistications.

00:59:02.220 --> 00:59:04.200
These require
parallel computing--

00:59:04.200 --> 00:59:05.670
it's a big program--

00:59:05.670 --> 00:59:08.840
takes 20, 30 hours to compute.

00:59:08.840 --> 00:59:11.250
And I think, as far as
computing is concerned,

00:59:11.250 --> 00:59:12.390
it is the state of the art.

00:59:12.390 --> 00:59:15.810
Now, of course, reality
may be different.

00:59:15.810 --> 00:59:17.270
AUDIENCE: How do
you throttle it?

00:59:17.270 --> 00:59:17.510
RUBBIA: What?

00:59:17.510 --> 00:59:18.986
AUDIENCE: How do you
throttle the engine?

00:59:18.986 --> 00:59:20.319
Do you change just the gas flow?

00:59:20.319 --> 00:59:20.960
Or do you--

00:59:20.960 --> 00:59:23.418
RUBBIA: You-- first
of all, this thing

00:59:23.418 --> 00:59:24.960
has very strong
negative coefficient,

00:59:24.960 --> 00:59:26.400
temperature coefficient.

00:59:26.400 --> 00:59:29.370
Therefore, by putting the
position of your control rods,

00:59:29.370 --> 00:59:33.200
you somehow define
the temperature.

00:59:33.200 --> 00:59:36.110
Define the temperature
of your device,

00:59:36.110 --> 00:59:43.220
because, when it cools down, it
gets too high a cross-section,

00:59:43.220 --> 00:59:44.720
and therefore k grows.

00:59:44.720 --> 00:59:50.030
But then, when it gets hot, it
stops, et cetera, et cetera.

00:59:50.030 --> 00:59:53.090
So essentially there
is a very, very strong

00:59:53.090 --> 00:59:55.670
negative temperature
coefficient.

00:59:55.670 --> 00:59:58.280
And indeed, you can
write the temperature

00:59:58.280 --> 00:59:59.330
on your control rods.

00:59:59.330 --> 01:00:01.562
And, depending how deeply
you're putting in and out,

01:00:01.562 --> 01:00:03.770
you will fix the temperature
operation of the system.

01:00:03.770 --> 01:00:06.110
So you can control
the amount of power.

01:00:06.110 --> 01:00:07.840
Now, the second
variable you have

01:00:07.840 --> 01:00:09.710
is the amount of gas you get in.

01:00:09.710 --> 01:00:12.770
And now, if you put
in a lot of gas,

01:00:12.770 --> 01:00:15.800
it will go in and out
rapidly, and therefore it

01:00:15.800 --> 01:00:18.910
will change substantially
the operation.

01:00:18.910 --> 01:00:21.580
So the second control
you have is that one.

01:00:21.580 --> 01:00:25.390
With the two things, you
can, in fact, quite well

01:00:25.390 --> 01:00:29.840
cover thrust factors
about factor 10.

01:00:29.840 --> 01:00:32.240
However, when you
look at the mission,

01:00:32.240 --> 01:00:35.750
you realize that this
kind of variable thrust

01:00:35.750 --> 01:00:40.440
or variable in specific
impulse or whatever you want,

01:00:40.440 --> 01:00:42.800
it's more a sophistication
for mathematicians

01:00:42.800 --> 01:00:44.285
than the reality.

01:00:44.285 --> 01:00:47.550
When you get there, you
go to a flat-on period.

01:00:47.550 --> 01:00:51.040
And so I would expect the
95% to 99% of the time

01:00:51.040 --> 01:00:53.173
the thing will run on and off.

01:00:53.173 --> 01:00:55.340
What you do in the mission,
you start, first of all,

01:00:55.340 --> 01:00:56.570
pushing it.

01:00:56.570 --> 01:01:00.330
And then, when you get close to
Mars, you have to turn round,

01:01:00.330 --> 01:01:04.460
and then you have to slow
down, to catch the system.

01:01:04.460 --> 01:01:07.340
So the period in which
the engine is on is off.

01:01:07.340 --> 01:01:11.960
But essentially you don't
have that much room for--

01:01:11.960 --> 01:01:15.043
doesn't seem necessary to have
high sophistication, in the way

01:01:15.043 --> 01:01:15.960
the engine is running.

01:01:15.960 --> 01:01:18.890
You're not running
it downtown Boston.

01:01:18.890 --> 01:01:20.460
[INAUDIBLE]

01:01:20.460 --> 01:01:25.820
And this is only for the
transplanetary travel.

01:01:25.820 --> 01:01:27.650
Then, when you want to--

01:01:27.650 --> 01:01:29.260
then you stay on a--

01:01:29.260 --> 01:01:30.940
you have essentially
two standby orbit.

01:01:30.940 --> 01:01:33.620
One is the present
space station,

01:01:33.620 --> 01:01:36.410
and the other one should
be identical, over Mars.

01:01:36.410 --> 01:01:39.950
And then you have
transfer between the two.

01:01:39.950 --> 01:01:42.110
And most of it is
reusable, by the way.

01:01:42.110 --> 01:01:42.913
Yes, sir.

01:01:42.913 --> 01:01:45.080
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] couple
questions [INAUDIBLE]..

01:01:54.660 --> 01:01:55.910
RUBBIA: "Convective transfer."

01:01:55.910 --> 01:01:56.660
What do you mean?

01:01:56.660 --> 01:01:56.970
Yes--

01:01:56.970 --> 01:01:57.260
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:01:57.260 --> 01:01:58.880
AUDIENCE: Ordinary process
of transferring heat

01:01:58.880 --> 01:01:59.630
from a gas to a--

01:01:59.630 --> 01:02:01.507
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:02:01.507 --> 01:02:03.590
RUBBIA: [INAUDIBLE] the
conductivity and radiation

01:02:03.590 --> 01:02:05.840
conductivity are in the program.

01:02:05.840 --> 01:02:07.790
But actually the
conductivity of gas, this

01:02:07.790 --> 01:02:11.060
doesn't seem to be very
important, very strong.

01:02:11.060 --> 01:02:13.160
What happens is, the gas
starts moving very slowly

01:02:13.160 --> 01:02:17.690
from the wall and then moves
slowly towards the center,

01:02:17.690 --> 01:02:18.440
warming up.

01:02:18.440 --> 01:02:22.460
It takes about a second,
one second, to go from--

01:02:22.460 --> 01:02:23.970
through the system.

01:02:23.970 --> 01:02:26.990
So it's a very,
very slow motion.

01:02:26.990 --> 01:02:30.920
And the corrections coming
from heat conductivity

01:02:30.920 --> 01:02:31.910
are not that important.

01:02:31.910 --> 01:02:34.130
In the long run,
they are irrelevant

01:02:34.130 --> 01:02:37.280
except for the heat,
which it goes to the wall.

01:02:37.280 --> 01:02:39.780
Because, if inside the gas
there is some heat exchange,

01:02:39.780 --> 01:02:40.530
it doesn't matter.

01:02:40.530 --> 01:02:42.170
The important thing
is that you deliver

01:02:42.170 --> 01:02:43.779
the power inside the gas.

01:02:43.779 --> 01:02:45.154
AUDIENCE: And the
second question

01:02:45.154 --> 01:02:49.046
was, the lithium cools the
wall, and who cools the lithium?

01:02:49.046 --> 01:02:50.100
[INAUDIBLE]?

01:02:50.100 --> 01:02:50.860
RUBBIA: Ah.

01:02:50.860 --> 01:02:53.860
I have been a little bit too
quick, probably, on that,

01:02:53.860 --> 01:02:58.760
but I am very happy
to come back to it.

01:02:58.760 --> 01:03:08.400
What I was saying,
here, is that we

01:03:08.400 --> 01:03:12.810
have here a liquid lithium
inside this device, which

01:03:12.810 --> 01:03:14.890
is here.

01:03:14.890 --> 01:03:17.410
And the lithium boils.

01:03:17.410 --> 01:03:20.530
Volume of lithium
produces some, like in

01:03:20.530 --> 01:03:23.050
a boiling-water reactor,
boiling-lithium reactor,

01:03:23.050 --> 01:03:25.480
so you get vapor [INAUDIBLE].

01:03:25.480 --> 01:03:30.660
The lithium vapor
is separated here.

01:03:30.660 --> 01:03:33.980
Here, you separate
vapor from liquid.

01:03:33.980 --> 01:03:36.650
The rest of the liquid comes
back and circulates again

01:03:36.650 --> 01:03:39.320
into this, in this volume.

01:03:39.320 --> 01:03:45.590
And the gas is sent to the
irradiation panels, which

01:03:45.590 --> 01:03:49.080
are panels put all
around the engine,

01:03:49.080 --> 01:03:52.120
and which they are operating
about 1,200 degrees.

01:03:52.120 --> 01:03:53.918
So they give you about 300--

01:03:53.918 --> 01:03:55.710
what is it, 300 kilowatts
per square meter,

01:03:55.710 --> 01:03:57.040
or something like that.

01:03:57.040 --> 01:04:00.010
So they're about 1,000 square
meters-- something like that.

01:04:00.010 --> 01:04:04.860
And they are essentially
foils in which

01:04:04.860 --> 01:04:08.380
the lithium enters as a gas.

01:04:08.380 --> 01:04:11.730
It condenses on these tubes.

01:04:11.730 --> 01:04:14.760
And then it comes back
in terms of liquid back.

01:04:14.760 --> 01:04:16.380
The problem we
have seen there is

01:04:16.380 --> 01:04:19.940
that meteorites may punch
a hole into these walls.

01:04:19.940 --> 01:04:21.350
[LAUGHTER]

01:04:21.350 --> 01:04:21.850
Ha.

01:04:21.850 --> 01:04:22.740
It may happen.

01:04:22.740 --> 01:04:26.040
And therefore, you have to have
a system in which the system is

01:04:26.040 --> 01:04:27.180
sufficiently segmented.

01:04:27.180 --> 01:04:30.580
Ideally, you have some kind
of wall valves or whatever,

01:04:30.580 --> 01:04:34.210
which avoid that
kind of phenomenon.

01:04:34.210 --> 01:04:38.380
But, at the temperature of 1,200
degrees, to get rid of the heat

01:04:38.380 --> 01:04:40.542
is not an important problem.

01:04:40.542 --> 01:04:42.270
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
some electricity?

01:04:42.270 --> 01:04:42.540
RUBBIA: What?

01:04:42.540 --> 01:04:44.345
AUDIENCE: Don't you need some
electricity on a mission?

01:04:44.345 --> 01:04:46.050
RUBBIA: Ah, of course that's--

01:04:46.050 --> 01:04:46.920
I mean, this is--

01:04:46.920 --> 01:04:48.240
absolutely right.

01:04:48.240 --> 01:04:52.050
In this present approximation,
I'm saying, this heat is waste.

01:04:52.050 --> 01:04:55.230
In reality, having 1,000
degrees temperature,

01:04:55.230 --> 01:04:58.160
it's not a bad thing
to use, to do all kinds

01:04:58.160 --> 01:04:59.880
of different things with it.

01:04:59.880 --> 01:05:03.930
And therefore all the support,
the energy [INAUDIBLE],,

01:05:03.930 --> 01:05:06.207
whatever, will all
be taken out of that.

01:05:06.207 --> 01:05:08.040
And, by the way, there
are very nice devices

01:05:08.040 --> 01:05:11.640
which are developed
apparently nowadays in Russia,

01:05:11.640 --> 01:05:14.070
which are thermionic devices.

01:05:14.070 --> 01:05:16.080
They're using a very
narrow gap, and they put

01:05:16.080 --> 01:05:18.960
a thermionic emission effects.

01:05:18.960 --> 01:05:21.705
And you make
[INAUDIBLE],, essentially.

01:05:21.705 --> 01:05:24.800
You put gas in there, so
that you can get the high--

01:05:24.800 --> 01:05:26.520
the space charge is eliminated.

01:05:26.520 --> 01:05:30.550
And they take a lot of power,
[INAUDIBLE] those things.

01:05:30.550 --> 01:05:32.230
This would be ideal
for this device.

01:05:32.230 --> 01:05:33.800
You will have 1,000 degrees.

01:05:33.800 --> 01:05:38.040
It is like a cathode
of a vacuum tube.

01:05:38.040 --> 01:05:40.080
And it will emit electrons,
and those electron

01:05:40.080 --> 01:05:43.290
will give you one volt potential
or something like that.

01:05:43.290 --> 01:05:44.790
And then you will
be able to recover

01:05:44.790 --> 01:05:47.270
a fraction of the energy
directly on the panels,

01:05:47.270 --> 01:05:52.790
in form of secondary emission
thermionic type of devices.

01:05:56.512 --> 01:05:58.470
There is plenty of
possibility using that heat.

01:05:58.470 --> 01:06:00.020
It's not just garbage. .

01:06:00.020 --> 01:06:01.290
There was some question there.

01:06:01.290 --> 01:06:01.790
Yes, sir.

01:06:01.790 --> 01:06:06.123
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:06:06.123 --> 01:06:07.290
RUBBIA: I couldn't hear you.

01:06:07.290 --> 01:06:09.234
AUDIENCE: What would happen
to the fission fragment?

01:06:09.234 --> 01:06:10.206
Would [INAUDIBLE]?

01:06:10.206 --> 01:06:12.970
Or [INAUDIBLE] go with
the hydrogen gases?

01:06:12.970 --> 01:06:14.061
RUBBIA: What is that?

01:06:14.061 --> 01:06:16.420
AUDIENCE: What happens
to the fission fragment?

01:06:16.420 --> 01:06:17.100
RUBBIA: Okay.

01:06:17.100 --> 01:06:18.810
Very good question.

01:06:18.810 --> 01:06:22.740
First of all, Professor
Kerman will tell me,

01:06:22.740 --> 01:06:24.290
the fission fragment
are necessary,

01:06:24.290 --> 01:06:27.130
because without fission fragment
there are no delayed neutrons.

01:06:27.130 --> 01:06:28.588
And without delayed
neutrons, there

01:06:28.588 --> 01:06:30.905
is no reactor, because
the reactors are prompt.

01:06:30.905 --> 01:06:32.780
So a certain fraction
of the fission fragment

01:06:32.780 --> 01:06:34.220
have to stay inside the system.

01:06:34.220 --> 01:06:37.850
If you throw them all out,
you will have no possibility

01:06:37.850 --> 01:06:39.540
of controlling the reactor.

01:06:39.540 --> 01:06:41.390
Now, in reality,
as I said to you

01:06:41.390 --> 01:06:46.640
about the 34% of the energy is,
in fact, carried from the gas

01:06:46.640 --> 01:06:48.045
into the--

01:06:48.045 --> 01:06:49.670
from the fission
fragment into the gas.

01:06:49.670 --> 01:06:54.620
The remaining two thirds
of the fission fragments

01:06:54.620 --> 01:06:57.275
are, in fact, staying
inside the material.

01:06:57.275 --> 01:06:58.400
In principle, they should--

01:06:58.400 --> 01:07:01.150
Half will stay, if they
foil is infinitely thin.

01:07:01.150 --> 01:07:04.020
But if it's a little thicker,
you stop more of them.

01:07:04.020 --> 01:07:05.630
So about half of the
fission fragments

01:07:05.630 --> 01:07:09.470
will stay inside, implanted
inside, your foil,

01:07:09.470 --> 01:07:11.830
just as they do in
a normally fuel.

01:07:11.830 --> 01:07:14.450
In a normal fuel
reactor, they stay there,

01:07:14.450 --> 01:07:15.920
and they eventually
get eliminated.

01:07:15.920 --> 01:07:19.170
The other half will
go into the gas

01:07:19.170 --> 01:07:23.100
and will obviously be
exhausted in the environment.

01:07:23.100 --> 01:07:26.220
Now, obviously, that
is maybe a problem.

01:07:26.220 --> 01:07:28.373
Indeed, it's not a
problem, because we

01:07:28.373 --> 01:07:30.540
are talking about a relatively
small amount of fuel.

01:07:30.540 --> 01:07:33.770
We are talking about a few
kilograms, over the billion

01:07:33.770 --> 01:07:38.180
kilometers of transfer between
the Earth and Mars and return.

01:07:38.180 --> 01:07:42.770
We are talking about a very
high speed of emission.

01:07:42.770 --> 01:07:46.760
And the kind of radioactivity
garbage that there is in open

01:07:46.760 --> 01:07:49.900
space, it's so large, anyway,
that these kind of fission

01:07:49.900 --> 01:07:52.610
fragments-- nobody will even
be able to detect them--

01:07:52.610 --> 01:07:53.110
[LAUGHTER]

01:07:53.110 --> 01:07:54.840
--anyway.

01:07:54.840 --> 01:07:57.150
So I don't think they
represent a problem.

01:07:57.150 --> 01:08:00.830
By the way, also in Nerva
they are thrown out.

01:08:00.830 --> 01:08:07.390
You know, this thing goes off
at 35 kilometers a second.

01:08:07.390 --> 01:08:09.900
It's 100,000 kilometers an hour.

01:08:09.900 --> 01:08:12.078
That's the speed at which
this thing comes out.

01:08:12.078 --> 01:08:15.170
AUDIENCE: And the Martians
will be able to [INAUDIBLE]

01:08:15.170 --> 01:08:15.670
[LAUGHTER]

01:08:15.670 --> 01:08:18.160
RUBBIA: Right,
right, they will--

01:08:18.160 --> 01:08:18.660
good.

01:08:18.660 --> 01:08:20.649
There was another question?

01:08:20.649 --> 01:08:21.715
Yes, sir.

01:08:21.715 --> 01:08:23.590
AUDIENCE: Yeah, [INAUDIBLE]
the main problems

01:08:23.590 --> 01:08:26.490
with open-cycle systems
like this is not so much

01:08:26.490 --> 01:08:28.979
that the fission fragments
contaminate whatever,

01:08:28.979 --> 01:08:30.979
since we're talking about
space, but the fission

01:08:30.979 --> 01:08:34.300
fragments actually lower the
specific impulse of the system.

01:08:34.300 --> 01:08:36.319
RUBBIA: No, because
there are very few.

01:08:36.319 --> 01:08:38.263
Because, you see, the
average temperature

01:08:38.263 --> 01:08:40.180
of the gas, the average
temperature of the gas

01:08:40.180 --> 01:08:41.910
is 10,000 degrees.

01:08:41.910 --> 01:08:44.990
That means every atom
has 1 electrovolt.

01:08:44.990 --> 01:08:48.914
The fission fragment
is providing 100 MeV.

01:08:48.914 --> 01:08:50.200
Okay?

01:08:50.200 --> 01:08:59.490
Takes a lot 1-electrovolt atoms
for 100 MeV kinetic energy.

01:08:59.490 --> 01:09:02.270
There's a factor 10 to the 8.

01:09:02.270 --> 01:09:06.770
So the one fission fragment,
one fission fragment will heat

01:09:06.770 --> 01:09:11.460
[INAUDIBLE] hydrogen atoms--

01:09:11.460 --> 01:09:14.990
and energy conservation.

01:09:14.990 --> 01:09:18.310
And so you'll see that you have
1 electron volt outside there,

01:09:18.310 --> 01:09:22.540
but indeed you have 10
to the 8 to start with.

01:09:22.540 --> 01:09:24.700
So it's 10 to the 8 to
1, 10 to the 7 to 1--

01:09:24.700 --> 01:09:25.840
something like that.

01:09:25.840 --> 01:09:28.130
So there will be 1 in 10
to the 7, 1 in10 to the 8,

01:09:28.130 --> 01:09:30.173
which will be a
fission fragment.

01:09:30.173 --> 01:09:31.465
And that's a very small number.

01:09:31.465 --> 01:09:32.715
It doesn't contribute.

01:09:32.715 --> 01:09:33.812
There are ideas.

01:09:33.812 --> 01:09:36.020
People have proposed to use
fission fragment directly

01:09:36.020 --> 01:09:37.390
as a propulsion systems.

01:09:37.390 --> 01:09:39.890
There are ideas in which you
take the fission fragment, take

01:09:39.890 --> 01:09:43.010
the foil, don't put anything,
and let the fission fragment go

01:09:43.010 --> 01:09:44.359
into space.

01:09:44.359 --> 01:09:46.263
That will give you
a push, obviously.

01:09:46.263 --> 01:09:47.930
The problem is that
the fission fragment

01:09:47.930 --> 01:09:52.160
will have a very small
push, because they

01:09:52.160 --> 01:09:54.860
have a very high speed.

01:09:54.860 --> 01:09:59.840
And therefore they will give you
a very high specific impulse.

01:09:59.840 --> 01:10:01.350
They will be ideal,
for instance,

01:10:01.350 --> 01:10:04.410
to go to outside
the solar system.

01:10:04.410 --> 01:10:10.380
If you wanted to
go to a nearby sun,

01:10:10.380 --> 01:10:13.067
you'll need the
fission-fragment propulsion.

01:10:13.067 --> 01:10:14.900
In this case, you will
not put the hydrogen.

01:10:14.900 --> 01:10:16.250
You will just make
the system and let

01:10:16.250 --> 01:10:17.500
the fission fragment come out.

01:10:17.500 --> 01:10:22.150
They would come out with a speed
of 4% of the speed of light.

01:10:22.150 --> 01:10:23.800
And therefore they
will be optimal

01:10:23.800 --> 01:10:26.980
when the rocket will go
to 4% of speed of light.

01:10:26.980 --> 01:10:34.390
So it will make, in 20 times,
20 times the light distance.

01:10:34.390 --> 01:10:35.870
So you'll get there.

01:10:35.870 --> 01:10:40.270
So, if you are five light
years, 5 times 20, in 100 years

01:10:40.270 --> 01:10:44.260
you'll get to the
neighboring sun.

01:10:44.260 --> 01:10:45.850
You can make a
mission of that type.

01:10:45.850 --> 01:10:48.890
For that case, you will need
the fission fragment directly.

01:10:48.890 --> 01:10:50.133
It would work the same way.

01:10:50.133 --> 01:10:51.800
I mean, there's nothing
wrong with that.

01:10:51.800 --> 01:10:53.740
You will not put
hydrogen-- let them out.

01:10:53.740 --> 01:10:55.240
In fact, some other
people have even

01:10:55.240 --> 01:10:57.070
thought about it, in
the past, [INAUDIBLE]

01:10:57.070 --> 01:11:00.250
and so forth--
californium, and so forth.

01:11:00.250 --> 01:11:04.870
That will be good if you were
to have much higher speeds.

01:11:04.870 --> 01:11:08.900
But here you want to have
a relatively modest job.

01:11:08.900 --> 01:11:10.900
You want to fill the gap
between normal chemical

01:11:10.900 --> 01:11:15.290
and this kind of
vast propulsions.

01:11:15.290 --> 01:11:19.380
So you need a lot of stuff,
with a relatively small speed,

01:11:19.380 --> 01:11:23.110
to get sufficient thrust
with a modest power,

01:11:23.110 --> 01:11:25.870
because the power
goes like MV squared.

01:11:25.870 --> 01:11:27.490
The thrust goes MV.

01:11:27.490 --> 01:11:31.270
So, if you have very high
speed, for a given power,

01:11:31.270 --> 01:11:33.250
you get a little thrust.

01:11:33.250 --> 01:11:35.787
But you get a
high-quality thrust.

01:11:35.787 --> 01:11:37.120
So eventually you could do that.

01:11:37.120 --> 01:11:39.973
You just run your
system with empty

01:11:39.973 --> 01:11:41.390
and let those
fission fragment go.

01:11:41.390 --> 01:11:44.350
And then you would get
very tiny, little thrust.

01:11:44.350 --> 01:11:46.480
But they will go for--

01:11:46.480 --> 01:11:50.920
will be on, even
if your spacecraft

01:11:50.920 --> 01:11:55.140
gets speeds which are very close
to the one of speed of light.

01:11:55.140 --> 01:11:58.920
It's somewhere in between that
and antimatter, if you like.

01:11:58.920 --> 01:12:01.175
In my view, is-- but
it's very simple,

01:12:01.175 --> 01:12:02.550
as an apparatus,
because you just

01:12:02.550 --> 01:12:07.210
have a layer which emits
fission fragment in space.

01:12:07.210 --> 01:12:08.680
So it can be very reliable.

01:12:08.680 --> 01:12:11.890
You can think of having the damn
thing running for a long time.

01:12:11.890 --> 01:12:12.780
Yes, sir.

01:12:12.780 --> 01:12:20.500
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
you say about 1/2 to 2/3

01:12:20.500 --> 01:12:23.970
of the fission fragments
stay in the wall.

01:12:23.970 --> 01:12:27.970
But only about 3% of the
[INAUDIBLE] is facing the wall.

01:12:27.970 --> 01:12:28.470
[INAUDIBLE]

01:12:28.470 --> 01:12:29.387
RUBBIA: Who said that?

01:12:29.387 --> 01:12:30.220
Who said that?

01:12:30.220 --> 01:12:33.390
AUDIENCE: Well, there were 1
and 1/2 megawatt in the hydrogen

01:12:33.390 --> 01:12:35.225
and [INAUDIBLE]--

01:12:35.225 --> 01:12:36.140
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:12:36.140 --> 01:12:37.223
RUBBIA: --different power.

01:12:37.223 --> 01:12:39.940
[INAUDIBLE] This is the
power which is coming back

01:12:39.940 --> 01:12:41.740
because of radiation.

01:12:41.740 --> 01:12:44.380
There was a question
Arthur Kerman had--

01:12:44.380 --> 01:12:48.910
what happens of the light
emitted by the hot plasma--

01:12:48.910 --> 01:12:50.800
how much there is
there, and how much

01:12:50.800 --> 01:12:52.900
is the effect on the walls?

01:12:52.900 --> 01:12:54.070
And that is a small effect.

01:12:54.070 --> 01:12:55.310
AUDIENCE: Well, let me change
the question [INAUDIBLE]

01:12:55.310 --> 01:12:57.970
how much is the total
amount of [INAUDIBLE]

01:12:57.970 --> 01:12:59.433
on the walls [INAUDIBLE]?

01:12:59.433 --> 01:13:00.850
RUBBIA: Well, I
was explaining you

01:13:00.850 --> 01:13:03.400
that I am taking the
same kind of situation

01:13:03.400 --> 01:13:05.560
as a normal reactor.

01:13:05.560 --> 01:13:06.790
Let's take a normal reactor.

01:13:06.790 --> 01:13:09.410
In a normal reactor,
you have fuel rods.

01:13:09.410 --> 01:13:12.290
The fuel rod produces
about 200 or 300 watts

01:13:12.290 --> 01:13:14.320
per square centimeter.

01:13:14.320 --> 01:13:17.140
That rod is immersed
inside the sodium--

01:13:17.140 --> 01:13:19.330
for instance, Superphénix--
or other liquid,

01:13:19.330 --> 01:13:21.010
if you have different machines.

01:13:21.010 --> 01:13:22.930
And this liquid
takes away the heat.

01:13:22.930 --> 01:13:24.340
Correct?

01:13:24.340 --> 01:13:28.390
Now, imagine that the
rod now is changed.

01:13:28.390 --> 01:13:32.290
I remove the inside, the
fuel which is inside,

01:13:32.290 --> 01:13:34.300
and I replace it
with an empty tube,

01:13:34.300 --> 01:13:37.240
in which I put some
hydrogen-- that's my business.

01:13:37.240 --> 01:13:40.000
And I transform
the fuel from being

01:13:40.000 --> 01:13:43.780
solid all the way to the center
just to be on the periphery.

01:13:43.780 --> 01:13:45.563
If I put that fuel
on the periphery,

01:13:45.563 --> 01:13:47.230
my cooling will go
much better because I

01:13:47.230 --> 01:13:49.420
will have no conductivity
problem inside the fuel,

01:13:49.420 --> 01:13:52.810
itself, rod, but the amount
of power per unit of surface

01:13:52.810 --> 01:13:55.650
is the same as the previous one.

01:13:55.650 --> 01:13:58.440
So, in the same way I was
able to run a reactor,

01:13:58.440 --> 01:14:00.570
with that power density,
I'll be able to run

01:14:00.570 --> 01:14:04.110
this, quote-unquote,
"reactor" with empty rods.

01:14:04.110 --> 01:14:04.950
I empty the rods.

01:14:04.950 --> 01:14:09.050
I go from two-dimensional to
three-dimension to a surface.

01:14:09.050 --> 01:14:10.670
Do you follow me?

01:14:10.670 --> 01:14:12.590
So all the thermodynamics
is already solved,

01:14:12.590 --> 01:14:15.440
by any ordinary reactor guide.

01:14:15.440 --> 01:14:19.680
I would calculate my
own engine in such a way

01:14:19.680 --> 01:14:21.140
as if I have no hydrogen--

01:14:21.140 --> 01:14:23.360
[INAUDIBLE] turn off the
hydrogen, accidentally--

01:14:23.360 --> 01:14:26.008
and [INAUDIBLE] all the power
goes to the fission fragment,

01:14:26.008 --> 01:14:27.800
I will have enough
cooling to make it work,

01:14:27.800 --> 01:14:30.060
as it is in a normal
reactor, where

01:14:30.060 --> 01:14:33.633
all the power goes into fission
fragment and becomes heating.

01:14:33.633 --> 01:14:35.050
So, you see, there
is a continuity

01:14:35.050 --> 01:14:37.552
between building a reactor
and building this machine.

01:14:37.552 --> 01:14:39.010
If any, when you
make this machine,

01:14:39.010 --> 01:14:42.690
you are better off than
you are in a reactor.

01:14:42.690 --> 01:14:43.190
What?

01:14:43.190 --> 01:14:45.970
AUDIENCE: Do you have cooling
inside the [INAUDIBLE]??

01:14:45.970 --> 01:14:47.470
RUBBIA: No, but I
forget about that.

01:14:47.470 --> 01:14:50.260
I design a machine in such a way
that, even if there is no gas,

01:14:50.260 --> 01:14:51.658
I don't want to have a meltdown.

01:14:54.460 --> 01:14:58.270
And this is famous 200 watt
per square centimeters.

01:14:58.270 --> 01:15:00.750
Which is the normal power
in Superphénix, in Phénix,

01:15:00.750 --> 01:15:04.530
in other machines which
are run in the world.

01:15:04.530 --> 01:15:07.220
Cooled with metal.

01:15:07.220 --> 01:15:09.560
So, you know, it's like
you replace your rod

01:15:09.560 --> 01:15:10.620
with an empty rod.

01:15:10.620 --> 01:15:12.745
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] in
the end, half of the power

01:15:12.745 --> 01:15:15.040
has to be radiated
away [INAUDIBLE]

01:15:15.040 --> 01:15:17.030
RUBBIA: I am designing
a machine in such a way

01:15:17.030 --> 01:15:19.775
that the full power
can be dissipated away

01:15:19.775 --> 01:15:22.380
and still the machine works.

01:15:22.380 --> 01:15:24.030
Because if I switch
off the hydrogen,

01:15:24.030 --> 01:15:27.290
then all the fission
fragment will return--

01:15:27.290 --> 01:15:30.602
inside the layer,
on the other side.

01:15:30.602 --> 01:15:32.506
And I have to be
prepared for that.

01:15:32.506 --> 01:15:34.886
AUDIENCE: I think I see
where the confusion is,

01:15:34.886 --> 01:15:37.028
in that you're talking
about fission fragments

01:15:37.028 --> 01:15:39.646
and saying if one
third of them heats

01:15:39.646 --> 01:15:42.622
the gas to these
enormous energies,

01:15:42.622 --> 01:15:44.080
then the other two
thirds should be

01:15:44.080 --> 01:15:46.525
heating the walls of these
enormous temperatures, as well.

01:15:46.525 --> 01:15:47.650
Is that what you're saying?

01:15:47.650 --> 01:15:48.275
RUBBIA: No, no.

01:15:48.275 --> 01:15:56.403
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:15:56.403 --> 01:15:57.820
RUBBIA: But the
gas is-- the point

01:15:57.820 --> 01:16:00.880
is, they stop in the gas
20 times more efficiently.

01:16:00.880 --> 01:16:04.720
The point is that the
gas is very viscous,

01:16:04.720 --> 01:16:08.680
is very effective in
stopping the fission

01:16:08.680 --> 01:16:10.720
fragments, while
the other is not.

01:16:10.720 --> 01:16:12.190
Do you see the
difference in range?

01:16:12.190 --> 01:16:13.990
It's a factor 20 to 1.

01:16:13.990 --> 01:16:16.140
So the power density in
the gas is very high.

01:16:18.910 --> 01:16:22.490
This way, you get
megawatts per gram,

01:16:22.490 --> 01:16:25.120
with 200 watts per square
centimeter on the surface.

01:16:25.120 --> 01:16:26.980
Yes, sir.

01:16:26.980 --> 01:16:28.840
AUDIENCE: What are
the typical residence

01:16:28.840 --> 01:16:31.400
times for the neutrons
in the diffractor?

01:16:31.400 --> 01:16:32.530
RUBBIA: Can't hear you.

01:16:32.530 --> 01:16:34.197
AUDIENCE: What are
the typical residence

01:16:34.197 --> 01:16:37.350
times for the neutrons in the
diffractive medium, outside?

01:16:37.350 --> 01:16:41.810
RUBBIA: In this thing, they last
about 30 or 50 milliseconds.

01:16:41.810 --> 01:16:44.170
They are acoustic.

01:16:44.170 --> 01:16:47.920
You could hear them
coming and going.

01:16:47.920 --> 01:16:50.830
You know, they just go for--

01:16:50.830 --> 01:16:55.722
you know, they go about 300
or 400 times, back and forth.

01:16:55.722 --> 01:16:57.430
They're traveling a
kilometer per second.

01:16:57.430 --> 01:16:59.710
That's [INAUDIBLE]
thermal speed.

01:16:59.710 --> 01:17:02.097
So, 1,000 meters
per second-- sorry,

01:17:02.097 --> 01:17:03.430
1,000 meters per second-- right?

01:17:03.430 --> 01:17:04.472
Is the speed of neutrons.

01:17:04.472 --> 01:17:06.520
Right?

01:17:06.520 --> 01:17:10.060
They go 3 meters, each crossing.

01:17:10.060 --> 01:17:13.590
So 100 times is 300 meters.

01:17:13.590 --> 01:17:19.260
So 1,000 meters per second, 300
meters, is about 0.3 seconds.

01:17:19.260 --> 01:17:20.700
So those neutrons stay forever.

01:17:25.500 --> 01:17:26.440
They stay for a--

01:17:26.440 --> 01:17:28.183
You could hear it!

01:17:28.183 --> 01:17:30.350
You get the neutron in, and
when it comes out it say

01:17:30.350 --> 01:17:30.920
"bingo-bingo."

01:17:30.920 --> 01:17:32.150
You can hear the two
[INAUDIBLE] sounds.

01:17:32.150 --> 01:17:34.192
You should be able to send
two sounds separately,

01:17:34.192 --> 01:17:35.310
like in sonar.

01:17:35.310 --> 01:17:37.310
AUDIENCE: One last question.

01:17:37.310 --> 01:17:37.968
RUBBIA: Yes.

01:17:37.968 --> 01:17:41.740
AUDIENCE: Could you
review the [INAUDIBLE]

01:17:41.740 --> 01:17:44.650
slightly [INAUDIBLE] mission
profile, given this engine.

01:17:44.650 --> 01:17:48.730
Are you still talking
about [INAUDIBLE]??

01:17:48.730 --> 01:17:49.480
RUBBIA: No.

01:17:49.480 --> 01:17:52.410
[INAUDIBLE] This
is a good question.

01:17:52.410 --> 01:17:54.670
I think it's important
it's clarified.

01:17:54.670 --> 01:17:58.810
I don't believe you can send a
man on the Hohmann trajectory.

01:17:58.810 --> 01:18:01.920
One year to get there, 700 day--

01:18:01.920 --> 01:18:05.000
640 days to wait
for the good time.

01:18:05.000 --> 01:18:06.840
And then another
year to come back.

01:18:06.840 --> 01:18:09.470
Besides, if he's American every
year he has to pay his taxes.

01:18:09.470 --> 01:18:10.130
That's the law.

01:18:10.130 --> 01:18:10.970
[LAUGHTER]

01:18:10.970 --> 01:18:13.130
So he needs a special waiver.

01:18:13.130 --> 01:18:18.760
So anyway, so evidently
this is not possible.

01:18:18.760 --> 01:18:25.310
In the mission 3.0 of NASA,
one assumes that you get there

01:18:25.310 --> 01:18:28.507
and you manufacture
your own fuel there.

01:18:28.507 --> 01:18:30.590
So you get there, for the
first time in your life,

01:18:30.590 --> 01:18:32.720
in conditions which
are extremely harsh.

01:18:32.720 --> 01:18:39.530
You stay there for 600 days,
which is two years, and--

01:18:39.530 --> 01:18:41.090
in little boxes.

01:18:41.090 --> 01:18:43.610
And then you have to
make your own fuel.

01:18:43.610 --> 01:18:46.460
And if you need an extra
gasket, spare parts

01:18:46.460 --> 01:18:47.795
will be hard to find.

01:18:47.795 --> 01:18:49.170
[LAUGHTER]

01:18:49.170 --> 01:18:49.670
Correct?

01:18:49.670 --> 01:18:50.700
And then you come back.

01:18:50.700 --> 01:18:52.370
You stay another--
and by the way,

01:18:52.370 --> 01:18:53.840
also, there's a
fundamental point

01:18:53.840 --> 01:18:56.480
which has to do with radiation.

01:18:56.480 --> 01:18:58.790
We have calculated the
amount of radiation dose

01:18:58.790 --> 01:19:03.230
that you get by staying
there, and it's very large.

01:19:03.230 --> 01:19:08.200
The fact that Mars has a
very thin amount of air,

01:19:08.200 --> 01:19:09.950
it enhances the
radiation, because you get

01:19:09.950 --> 01:19:12.630
a maximum of the cosmic rays.

01:19:12.630 --> 01:19:14.270
And even if you go
underground, you

01:19:14.270 --> 01:19:16.700
have to go underground
something like 5, 10 meters

01:19:16.700 --> 01:19:18.750
before you can get rid
of the whole thing.

01:19:18.750 --> 01:19:23.750
And, in fact, you get
skyshine from the soil.

01:19:23.750 --> 01:19:24.650
You sit there.

01:19:24.650 --> 01:19:27.620
All these cosmic rays
go inside the soil,

01:19:27.620 --> 01:19:28.880
and then they come back--

01:19:28.880 --> 01:19:32.250
nice neutrons, which
you get into your body

01:19:32.250 --> 01:19:33.520
and so forth and so on.

01:19:33.520 --> 01:19:37.130
The dose, it's enormous.

01:19:37.130 --> 01:19:40.490
It's larger than the
Chernobyl firemen.

01:19:40.490 --> 01:19:45.270
So the risk of a very long trip
is primarily radiation hazard,

01:19:45.270 --> 01:19:46.440
in my view.

01:19:46.440 --> 01:19:48.320
And it's my view, it's
very much of the limit

01:19:48.320 --> 01:19:50.480
of what you can accept.

01:19:50.480 --> 01:19:52.920
50% survival probability,
stuff like that.

01:19:55.940 --> 01:19:57.590
Already on the moon
there was a lot

01:19:57.590 --> 01:19:59.270
of radiation given
to those astronauts.

01:19:59.270 --> 01:20:01.770
People don't underline that,
but that was a very short trip.

01:20:01.770 --> 01:20:04.650
Here, we're talking about
three years, four years,

01:20:04.650 --> 01:20:06.910
five years away from home.

01:20:06.910 --> 01:20:11.280
Now, obviously, the question is,
do you need a man over there?

01:20:11.280 --> 01:20:13.580
In my view, for many things
you don't need a man.

01:20:13.580 --> 01:20:15.770
You can send a test,
a device, et cetera.

01:20:15.770 --> 01:20:17.640
But those machinery
will be so complicated

01:20:17.640 --> 01:20:19.390
that you need somebody
to go and fix them.

01:20:19.390 --> 01:20:20.598
Look at the Hubble telescope.

01:20:20.598 --> 01:20:23.630
If you don't send the guy up
there to change the window,

01:20:23.630 --> 01:20:25.970
it will not be
possible to repair it.

01:20:25.970 --> 01:20:29.360
And similar thing will
happen also over to Mars.

01:20:29.360 --> 01:20:31.970
So Mars as a planet is a
very complicated thing.

01:20:31.970 --> 01:20:34.310
You have to do an enormous
amount of exploration,

01:20:34.310 --> 01:20:37.200
to do that, and lots of
experiments, stable studies,

01:20:37.200 --> 01:20:40.080
and not just go and
touch, touch and go.

01:20:43.300 --> 01:20:46.145
It's a real planet with a
complicated story and so forth

01:20:46.145 --> 01:20:47.020
and so on, et cetera.

01:20:47.020 --> 01:20:49.480
So you will have a lot
of equipment there, which

01:20:49.480 --> 01:20:51.210
will run more or
less automatically

01:20:51.210 --> 01:20:57.020
but will need now and then a
man to fix it, to install it,

01:20:57.020 --> 01:20:58.550
to modify it, et cetera.

01:20:58.550 --> 01:21:03.080
So you need presence of man in
space but not total presence--

01:21:03.080 --> 01:21:05.680
only in a short period of time.

01:21:05.680 --> 01:21:09.860
So we are devising a mission
in which you go there

01:21:09.860 --> 01:21:11.090
on a very short track.

01:21:11.090 --> 01:21:13.700
You go there in 100, 150 days.

01:21:13.700 --> 01:21:16.460
You stay there for a
limited period time,

01:21:16.460 --> 01:21:17.550
with no time window.

01:21:17.550 --> 01:21:20.600
So, if today you cannot do
it, if it's a dust tempest

01:21:20.600 --> 01:21:23.270
or whatever, you can
go back tomorrow.

01:21:23.270 --> 01:21:24.410
And then you come back.

01:21:24.410 --> 01:21:27.410
And then this you could
do with the periodicity

01:21:27.410 --> 01:21:32.690
which is 740 days, which is the
superperiodicity of the system.

01:21:32.690 --> 01:21:35.120
In the meantime,
you send the cargo,

01:21:35.120 --> 01:21:38.040
which can take all
the time to get there,

01:21:38.040 --> 01:21:41.540
with a lot of stuff, a lot of
equipment, which you install

01:21:41.540 --> 01:21:42.210
and you operate.

01:21:42.210 --> 01:21:45.380
So the idea is that you have
cargoes which bring the largest

01:21:45.380 --> 01:21:48.440
amount of material [INAUDIBLE]
the specific impulse is useful,

01:21:48.440 --> 01:21:50.180
because it reduces the--

01:21:50.180 --> 01:21:53.150
increases the efficiency
of the payload.

01:21:53.150 --> 01:21:55.680
And you get there with
a substantial payload,

01:21:55.680 --> 01:21:57.460
and then you leave it there.

01:21:57.460 --> 01:21:59.000
Essentially, it's dead.

01:21:59.000 --> 01:22:00.320
It never comes back.

01:22:00.320 --> 01:22:03.170
The engine, the TUG,
comes back empty,

01:22:03.170 --> 01:22:05.880
and the rest stays there.

01:22:05.880 --> 01:22:08.660
And then you send
a human mission

01:22:08.660 --> 01:22:11.900
periodically to update the
system, to improve the system,

01:22:11.900 --> 01:22:13.450
to collect the data,
to fix whatever

01:22:13.450 --> 01:22:15.450
has to be fixed, to
take the decision,

01:22:15.450 --> 01:22:17.620
but on a short period of time.

01:22:17.620 --> 01:22:23.550
And that implies about 1,500
days to stay there and 150 days

01:22:23.550 --> 01:22:25.173
to come back and forth.

01:22:25.173 --> 01:22:27.090
This is possible, with
these kind of engines--

01:22:27.090 --> 01:22:30.890
is impossible with the chemical.

01:22:30.890 --> 01:22:32.720
That from Braun
is already proven.

01:22:32.720 --> 01:22:35.660
It's marginally possible--
probably impossible-- as well,

01:22:35.660 --> 01:22:38.427
also, with Nerva's.

01:22:38.427 --> 01:22:40.010
The other problem
is also the question

01:22:40.010 --> 01:22:43.670
of putting in space an amount
of fissionable material.

01:22:43.670 --> 01:22:50.560
Here, americium is very similar
to plutonium 238, as I said.

01:22:50.560 --> 01:22:53.420
Americium is one third of the
toxicity of plutonium 238,

01:22:53.420 --> 01:22:55.640
roughly the same lifetime.

01:22:55.640 --> 01:22:57.550
And so we are bringing
in a hazard which

01:22:57.550 --> 01:23:01.630
is comparable to this
generator already used,

01:23:01.630 --> 01:23:07.230
to put a ton of highly
fissile material in orbit.

01:23:07.230 --> 01:23:10.260
Well, there are presumably
a reactor in orbit,

01:23:10.260 --> 01:23:12.720
but they belong to security--

01:23:12.720 --> 01:23:17.720
you know, classified activities
of some unspecified country.

01:23:17.720 --> 01:23:20.690
But, in practice, if you
make a thing like this,

01:23:20.690 --> 01:23:24.440
you can go with a ton of
uranium and hope the people will

01:23:24.440 --> 01:23:25.830
let you get away with it.

01:23:25.830 --> 01:23:27.830
MODERATOR: I'd love to
entertain more questions,

01:23:27.830 --> 01:23:29.888
but we have to close
things down [INAUDIBLE]..

01:23:29.888 --> 01:23:31.430
First, let's thank
our speaker again.

01:23:31.430 --> 01:23:32.180
RUBBIA: Thank you.

01:23:32.180 --> 01:23:37.480
[APPLAUSE]

